window.__WIKI_INDEX__ = [{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"Hermes Agent — Fresh Engineering Wiki","a":"","x":"Hermes Agent is a self-improving, multi-platform AI agent harness from Nous Research (MIT-licensed). It wraps any chat model in everything it needs to act in the world — a terminal, a filesystem, web and code execution, memory, and a gateway onto messaging platforms — and drives every user message to completion through one provider-agnostic turn loop. This wiki is a from-the-code reference: how Hermes is built, what makes it work, and which ideas transfer to any agent harness. Every claim is a path:line citation into this repository at commit 42a4c91c4 ."},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"What Hermes is","a":"what","x":"What Hermes is The entire system is a set of thin drivers around one agent core: the AIAgent class ( run_agent.py:412 ) and its single production turn loop, run_conversation ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1704 ). The CLI, the TUI, the multi-platform gateway daemon, the cron scheduler, the ACP adapter and the batch runner all feed the same loop — they differ only in how messages get in and how replies flow out. There is no second code path. It is provider-agnostic by design, speaking to ~40 backends through four API dialects (OpenAI ChatCompletion, Anthropic Messages, OpenAI Responses/Codex, Bedrock Converse) — including subscription-OAuth backends like ChatGPT Plus and Claude Pro/Max — with provider differences quarantined inside per-dialect transports so the loop body stays uniform. And it is genuinely self-improving: it creates skills from experience, patches them during use, nudges itself to persist knowledge, and searches its own past conversations. The core is deliberately simple, the edges deliberately paranoid. The loop head is a plain synchronous while (api_call_count < max_iterations and iteration_budget.remaining > 0) ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1922 ). Almost everything else in the repo is scaffolding to feed, protect, persist and observe that loop — and almost every non-obvious choice traces back to protecting the context window, surviving rate limits, or making stat"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"What's in this wiki","a":"pages","x":"What's in this wiki Architecture & Internals The one shared core, the entry points and process model, the gateway inbound/outbound pipeline, session keying and the SQLite state DB, subagent delegation, the execute_code RPC, and the four extension surfaces. Read the map → Inner & Outer Loops The outer loop that owns a session — intake, steering, interrupts, nudges, cron, idle lifecycle — and the inner loop that owns a turn, with the actual loop bodies and the ordering invariants that keep them safe. Read the loops → LLM Kernels & Adapters The client/adapter layer where inference, tool-calling and streaming execute per dialect — which SDKs are wrapped, how requests and tool schemas convert, how streaming and delta assembly work, prompt caching and reasoning params. Read the kernels → Providers & Subscription Tiers The provider catalog and, one by one, the subscription-contract integrations — Claude Pro/Max, ChatGPT/Codex, Grok, Copilot, Qwen, MiniMax, Nous — with OAuth flows, token stores, refresh, and client-identity spoofing. Read the registry → Messaging Channels How the chat channels are built and set up — the quickest, cleanest paths for Telegram, WhatsApp (Baileys & Cloud API) and Twilio SMS, plus Discord, Slack and Signal, with real config keys. Read the setup → Self-Adaptive Skills The workflows that turn work-over-time into skills: the complexity trigger, the out-of-band"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"A message, end to end","a":"bird","x":"A message, end to end A bird's-eye view of how a single chat message flows through the system, from platform SDK to reply:"},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"How to read the codebase","a":"reading","x":"How to read the codebase Walking these files in order forms a spine from the turn loop out to the providers: File Why it matters run_agent.py The AIAgent class ( run_agent.py:412 ) and its public surface — thin forwarders into the agent/ package. agent/conversation_loop.py The production turn loop: entry at agent/conversation_loop.py:1704 , budget loop at agent/conversation_loop.py:1922 , retry sub-loop at agent/conversation_loop.py:2741 . agent/tool_executor.py Concurrent/sequential/segmented tool execution ( agent/tool_executor.py:1048 ) with the start-order gate and daemon pool. agent/transports/ The four dialect transports — the format-conversion boundary ( agent/transports/base.py:1 ). gateway/run.py The gateway controller ( gateway/run.py:6547 ), message pipeline ( gateway/run.py:16237 ), turn runner ( gateway/run.py:27763 ). hermes_state_common.py The SQLite schema — sessions, messages, FTS5 ( hermes_state_common.py:249 ). hermes_cli/auth.py The provider registry and OAuth flows ( hermes_cli/auth.py:232 ). agent/background_review.py The self-improvement review fork ( agent/background_review.py:860 ) and its prompts."},{"u":"index.html","p":"Overview","t":"Provenance & grounding","a":"provenance","x":"Provenance & grounding This is a fresh, from-the-code wiki, written independently of the earlier wiki/ directory and the two generated reports at the repo root. Its content was re-derived from source by parallel code-exploration passes, and every path:line citation was spot-checked against the working tree at commit 42a4c91c4 (2026-08-19). Because line numbers drift as code changes, re-verify each <code class=\"cite\"> after large merges. The search index is regenerated with python3 wiki_fresh/build_search_index.py ; diagrams render offline from the vendored mermaid copy, with nothing loaded from a network. Caveat. Subscription-OAuth providers route real consumer subscriptions through agent traffic. Confirm such usage is within each provider's terms of service before relying on it in production; Hermes provides the transport, not the entitlement."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Architecture & Internals","a":"","x":"Hermes is a set of thin drivers wrapped around exactly one agent core: the AIAgent class and its single production turn loop. The CLI, the TUI, the multi-platform gateway daemon, the cron scheduler, the ACP adapter and the batch runner all feed the same loop — they differ only in how messages get in and how replies get out. This page maps that core, the gateway that fronts it, the SQLite state layer beneath it, and the internals of how agents spawn, steer and talk to each other."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"One shared core","a":"one-core","x":"One shared core The only agent class is AIAgent at run_agent.py:412 . Its constructor ( run_agent.py:435 ) and its public turn method run_conversation() ( run_agent.py:8339 ) are thin forwarders into the agent/ package: real construction happens in agent/agent_init.init_agent() ( agent/agent_init.py:493 ) and the real loop is agent/conversation_loop.run_conversation() ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1704 ). There is no second code path — every surface below drives this one function. Key insight. The loop head is a plain synchronous while (api_call_count < agent.max_iterations and agent.iteration_budget.remaining > 0) or agent._budget_grace_call at agent/conversation_loop.py:1922 . Everything else in the repo is scaffolding to feed, protect, persist, and observe that loop. The loop itself is dissected on the Turn Loops page."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Entry points & process model","a":"entry-points","x":"Entry points & process model Three console scripts are declared at pyproject.toml:372 : hermes → hermes_cli.main:main , hermes-agent → run_agent:main (raw Fire dev harness), and hermes-acp → acp_adapter.entry:main . Mode Entry Process model Interactive CLI hermes_cli/main.py:12228 → cmd_chat → cli.main() ( cli.py:20165 ) Single process. HermesCLI ( cli.py:4844 ) runs a process_loop() worker thread ( cli.py:19461 ) pulling from a queue; the agent turn runs synchronously on that thread. One-shot ( -z / -q ) run_oneshot() at hermes_cli/oneshot.py:170 One process, one turn, print, exit. Forces YOLO mode ( hermes_cli/oneshot.py:221 ) and declares a stateless channel so delegation runs inline instead of background ( hermes_cli/oneshot.py:226 ). TUI (Ink/React) Node spawns python -m tui_gateway.entry ( ui-tui/src/gatewayClient.ts:356 ) Two processes: Node TUI ↔ Python over newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdio; one daemon thread per turn ( tui_gateway/methods_prompt.py:803 ). Gateway daemon main() at gateway/run.py:30647 → start_gateway ( gateway/run.py:29946 ) One asyncio process, PID-locked ( gateway/run.py:29982 ); blocking agent work offloaded to a 10-worker ThreadPoolExecutor ( gateway/run.py:24197 ). Exits via os._exit to defeat wedged threads ( gateway/run.py:30775 ). ACP adapter acp_adapter/entry.py ; server HermesACPAgent ( acp_adapter/server.py:598 ) JSON-RPC over stdio; stdou"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Gateway: inbound path","a":"gateway-inbound","x":"Gateway: inbound path The gateway controller is GatewayRunner at gateway/run.py:6547 . Platform adapters subclass BasePlatformAdapter ( gateway/platforms/base.py:2890 ); built-ins live in gateway/platforms/ , plugin platforms register through PlatformRegistry ( gateway/platform_registry.py:232 ), and the Platform enum even fabricates pseudo-members for plugin platforms via _missing_() ( gateway/config.py:348 ). A message travels through a strictly ordered pipeline: Normalize. The adapter converts a platform update into a MessageEvent ( gateway/platforms/base.py:2300 ) carrying a SessionSource ( gateway/session.py:149 ) and calls handle_message() ( gateway/platforms/base.py:5981 ). Key. build_session_key(...) is derived right at the adapter boundary ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6006 ). Gate. A per-session busy guard ( _active_sessions , gateway/platforms/base.py:6033 ) either dispatches, merges the message into a pending FIFO ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6163 ), or — for /stop , /new , /reset — interrupts then dispatches ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6056 ). Spawn. _start_session_processing() installs the guard and creates an asyncio task per message ( gateway/platforms/base.py:5817 ). Route. The installed handler is GatewayRunner._handle_message ( gateway/run.py:16237 ): reset inherited context vars ( gateway/run.py:16265 ), resolve profile route (fail-closed, gateway/run.py:16289 "},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Session keying — the exact rules","a":"session-keying","x":"Session keying — the exact rules Single source of truth: build_session_key() at gateway/session.py:1090 , prefixed by a namespace from _session_key_namespace() ( gateway/session.py:1070 ) — agent:main for the default profile, agent:<profile> when multiplexing. DMs : <ns>:<platform>:dm[:scope_id][:chat_id][:thread_id] , falling back to the sender's user id when there is no chat id — the explicit anti-cross-user-bleed guard ( gateway/session.py:1149 ). Groups/channels/threads : chat-type keyed, with per-user isolation in groups on by default ( group_sessions_per_user=True , gateway/session.py:1092 ) while threads are shared by default ( gateway/session.py:1200 ). WhatsApp JID/LID identifiers are canonicalized on both sides ( gateway/session.py:1140 ); Discord auto-thread continuity hashes the channel-initiating message and its thread follow-ups to the same key via prospective_thread_id ( gateway/session.py:1177 ). So: sessions are per (profile, platform, chat, thread — and, in groups only, per user) . Not simply per-user, not simply per-channel."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Session store & the state DB","a":"session-store","x":"Session store & the state DB SessionStore ( gateway/session.py:1245 ) keeps routing metadata and transcripts in SQLite via SessionDB , falling back to legacy JSONL if SQLite is unavailable. It is synchronous and lock-guarded; the event loop reaches it through AsyncSessionStore , a __getattr__ proxy that wraps every call in asyncio.to_thread ( gateway/session.py:1221 ). The routing record is SessionEntry ( gateway/session.py:777 ): session_key → session_id plus origin, token/cost counters, suspension and model-override fields, persisted both to sessions.json and to the gateway_routing table ( hermes_state_common.py:373 ). The database itself is ~/.hermes/state.db ( hermes_state.py:379 ; home resolved by get_hermes_home() , hermes_constants.py:114 ), owned by SessionDB ( hermes_state.py:3093 ) with schema version 26 ( hermes_state_common.py:219 ) and DDL at hermes_state_common.py:249 : Table Purpose sessions One row per conversation: id, session_key, parent_session_id (subagent lineage), model + config, token/cost columns, compression state, profile, archived/pinned flags ( hermes_state_common.py:259 ). messages Transcript rows with role, content, tool_calls, reasoning, and three lifecycle flags — active , compacted , observed ( hermes_state_common.py:320 ). system_prompts Content-addressed prompt dedup, hash primary key ( hermes_state_common.py:254 ). session_model_usage Per (se"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Concurrency & queueing","a":"concurrency","x":"Concurrency & queueing Mechanism Where One asyncio task per inbound message gateway/platforms/base.py:5820 Per-session adapter guard ( _active_sessions: Dict[str, asyncio.Event] ) gateway/platforms/base.py:3054 Per-session pending FIFO with message merge gateway/platforms/base.py:3055 Cross-process durable turn lease per session_id gateway/turn_lease.py:152 Global cap on simultaneous active chat sessions gateway/run.py:9691 , rejection at gateway/run.py:17756 Blocking agent work off the event loop (10-worker pool) gateway/run.py:24197 LRU-cached AIAgent per session_key (preserves prompt cache) gateway/run.py:6883 Supervised background watchers, restart cap 5 gateway/run.py:13159 , gateway/run.py:13146 The supervised watcher set started at gateway/run.py:13050 includes session-expiry, session-stall, kanban notifier/dispatcher, platform reconnect, handoff, async-delegation, loop-wakeup, scale-to-zero and drain-control watchers — the gateway's \"outer outer loop\"."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Gateway: outbound path","a":"outbound","x":"Gateway: outbound path Two distinct paths carry output back to the user: Streaming, during the turn. GatewayStreamConsumer ( gateway/stream_consumer.py:156 ) is the single sink for typed events defined in gateway/stream_events.py:44 ( MessageChunk plus tool/lifecycle events). The contract is explicit: \"smart agent emits structured data, smart gateway decides delivery\" — and stream events are presentation-only, never persisted to history ( gateway/stream_events.py:26 ). Final reply / cron / notifications. DeliveryRouter.deliver() ( gateway/delivery.py:318 ) fans out over DeliveryTarget s parsed from strings like origin , telegram , telegram:123456:789 ( gateway/delivery.py:231 ), with a DeadTargetRegistry short-circuiting unreachable chats ( gateway/delivery.py:340 ). The handler return contract: Optional[Union[str, EphemeralReply]] — None means \"already delivered via streaming\" ( gateway/platforms/base.py:2782 ); stale replies are suppressed when the session was interrupted mid-turn ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6297 )."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Context management","a":"compaction","x":"Context management Context handling is pluggable behind ContextEngine(ABC) ( agent/context_engine.py:89 ) with the lifecycle contract on_session_start → update_from_response → should_compress → compress → on_session_end . The default engine is ContextCompressor ( agent/context_compressor.py:1848 ), whose documented algorithm is: prune old tool results (no LLM call), protect the head (system prompt + first exchange), protect the tail by token budget (~20K, agent/context_compressor.py:2099 ), summarize the middle with a structured LLM prompt, iteratively update that summary on later compactions. Durable anti-thrash guards persist compression-failure cooldowns and ineffective-compression streaks across restarts ( agent/context_compressor.py:2414 , agent/context_compressor.py:2350 ), and there is a micro-compaction rolling-summary path ( agent/context_compressor.py:1899 ). In-place compaction does not rotate the session id — the gateway re-baselines transcripts when it sees agent._last_compaction_in_place ( gateway/run.py:6318 ). Don't confuse the two compressors. trajectory_compressor.py at the repo root ( trajectory_compressor.py:332 ) is offline dataset post-processing for training-data trajectories — it pairs with batch_runner.py , not with live context management."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Subagents: delegate_task","a":"delegation","x":"Subagents: delegate_task Delegation is a tool the model calls: delegate_task(...) at tools/delegate_tool.py:3597 , dispatched agent-side through AIAgent._dispatch_delegate_task ( run_agent.py:8214 ). The central policy lives right there: _is_subagent = getattr(self, \"_delegate_depth\", 0) > 0 return _delegate_task(..., background=(not _is_subagent), parent_agent=self) Top-level delegations always run in the background ; a delegation issued by an orchestrator subagent stays synchronous, because the orchestrator needs results inside its own turn and does not own the gateway session an async result would route back to ( run_agent.py:8226 ). The model-facing background parameter is deliberately ignored ( run_agent.py:8236 ). Child construction & isolation _build_child_agent() ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1578 ) constructs a real AIAgent with: Depth/role : child_depth = parent._delegate_depth + 1 ; role degrades to leaf unless orchestration is explicitly allowed, and leaf children have delegate_task stripped from their toolset ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1278 ). Toolsets : inherited and intersected , never expanded — \"subagent must not gain tools the parent lacks\" ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1660 ). Context : an ephemeral child system prompt ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1173 ) with skip_context_files=True , skip_memory=True , platform=\"subagent\" , quiet_mode=True ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1950 ). P"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Live agent-to-agent I/O","a":"interaction","x":"Live agent-to-agent I/O Parent → child steering : steer_subagent() ( tools/delegate_tool.py:290 ) resolves the live registry record and calls AIAgent.steer(text) ( run_agent.py:3381 ), which appends the text to the child's last tool result at its next iteration boundary — the in-flight tool call is never cut. A steer that wins the race after the last delivery boundary is drained into the completion entry as missed_steer ( tools/delegate_tool.py:309 ). Parent → child interrupt : cooperative flag, recursing into grandchildren; hard interrupt on timeout ( tools/delegate_tool.py:266 , tools/delegate_tool.py:2814 ). Child → parent progress : _build_child_progress_callback ( tools/delegate_tool.py:1338 ) relays child tool events with subagent_id , parent_id , depth , model and toolsets threaded through, so a UI can reconstruct the live spawn tree; gateway mode batches tool names, CLI mode prints tree lines. Child text streaming : children stream via stream_callback=_relay_child_text ( tools/delegate_tool.py:2794 ). Model-facing control plane : delegate_task(action=\"list\"|\"steer\"|\"stop\") ( tools/delegate_tool.py:466 ), ownership enforced by walking the parent weakref chain ( tools/delegate_tool.py:380 ). Operator control plane : global spawn pause switch ( tools/delegate_tool.py:194 ) driven from the TUI /agents panel. Plugin-safe API : agent/subagent_lifecycle.py exposes immutable co"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"execute_code : RPC tool calls from Python scripts","a":"rpc","x":"execute_code : RPC tool calls from Python scripts The signature Hermes trick for collapsing multi-step pipelines: the model writes a Python script, and Hermes generates auto-stub modules whose functions call back into the parent process's real tool registry over RPC — so a fifty-call pipeline costs one inference turn and intermediate outputs never enter the context window. Server : _rpc_server_loop ( tools/code_execution_tool.py:652 ) runs on a thread in the parent process. Per request, in order: constant-time token check via secrets.compare_digest ( tools/code_execution_tool.py:709 ), tool allow-list, per-execution call limit, terminal blocked-params strip, then dispatch through the standard model_tools.handle_function_call(...) with stdout/stderr silenced ( tools/code_execution_tool.py:744 ). Transports : Unix domain socket by default; TCP on 127.0.0.1 for Windows; a file-based request/response transport for remote sandbox backends ( tools/code_execution_tool.py:1355 , tools/code_execution_tool.py:576 ). Child env : only HERMES_RPC_SOCKET + HERMES_RPC_TOKEN are handed over; everything else is scrubbed ( tools/code_execution_tool.py:1435 , allow-list at tools/code_execution_tool.py:147 ). Budget interaction : execute_code iterations are refunded to the IterationBudget ( agent/iteration_budget.py:28 ) — programmatic turns are free."},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Other multi-agent shapes","a":"other-shapes","x":"Other multi-agent shapes Shape What it is Where MoA (Mixture of Agents) Reference models fan out in parallel for one user turn; outputs are stably ordered, privacy-filtered, and fed to the primary model as advisor text. agent/moa_loop.py:795 Background review A fully separate AIAgent forked at end of turn to review the work; a new turn cancels a still-running review to avoid concurrent calls on the same credentials. run_agent.py:1805 , agent/conversation_loop.py:1771 Kanban workers Genuine subprocess agents ( hermes … chat -q ) dispatched from a task board, inside the gateway or via hermes kanban dispatch . hermes_cli/kanban_db.py:10876 Relay adapters Experimental \"gateway gateway\": one generic RelayAdapter fronted by connectors that hand over a wire-serializable CapabilityDescriptor at handshake — one adapter serves Discord/Telegram/Matrix/Signal without per-platform branching. gateway/relay/descriptor.py:1 Proxy mode The gateway short-circuits a turn to a remote Hermes API server when a proxy URL is configured. gateway/run.py:27794"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Hooks, events, middleware — four distinct surfaces","a":"extension","x":"Hooks, events, middleware — four distinct surfaces Plugin lifecycle hooks — the main surface. Allow-list VALID_HOOKS ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:156 ) spans tool, LLM, API, session, subagent ( subagent_start/stop ), gateway, approval, transcription and Kanban events. Front door: invoke_hook() ( hermes_cli/lifecycle.py:11 ), which fires first-party observability before plugins; has_hook() is the cheap short-circuit on every hot call site. Notably, pre_gateway_dispatch runs before auth so plugins can handle unauthorized senders, and slash-command observation deliberately excludes the running-agent intercept path so a hostile plugin cannot take away /stop ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:340 ). Plugin event bus — async and queued, distinct from hooks: PluginManager.subscribe() ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:3265 ), bounded queue, single ordered daemon worker, recursion-depth cap ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:5270 ). Middleware — \"observer hooks report what happened; middleware can change what happens\" ( hermes_cli/middleware.py:3 ). Kinds: tool_request , tool_execution , llm_request , llm_execution ; applied in the tool path at agent/tool_executor.py:540 . Shell hooks + gateway hook registry — a hooks: block in cli-config.yaml bridges shell scripts onto the plugin hook manager with first-use consent and shell=False execution ( agent/shell_hooks.py:1 ); separately, the gateway discovers filesystem hooks fro"},{"u":"architecture.html","p":"Architecture","t":"Cross-cutting: identity propagation","a":"identity","x":"Cross-cutting: identity propagation gateway/session_context.py replaced process-global os.environ[\"HERMES_SESSION_*\"] with ContextVar s precisely because concurrent gateway messages were overwriting each other's routing identity ( gateway/session_context.py:11 ). A _UNSET sentinel distinguishes \"never set here\" (fall back to env for CLI/cron) from \"explicitly cleared\" ( gateway/session_context.py:41 ). The consequences shape the whole codebase: _handle_message resets session vars on entry ( gateway/run.py:16255 ); every thread hand-off copies the context explicitly — executor offload ( gateway/run.py:24176 ), delegation fan-out ( tools/delegate_tool.py:3925 ), MoA workers ( agent/moa_loop.py:850 ); and async_delivery_supported() ( gateway/session_context.py:482 ) is what lets delegate_task choose background vs inline per surface. Transferable idea. \"Routing identity in ContextVars with explicit copies at every thread boundary\" is one of the cleanest solutions in the repo to the classic multi-tenant-agent identity-leak problem — see Transferables ."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner & Outer Loops","a":"","x":"The heart of Hermes is two loops: an outer loop that owns a session — intake, queueing, steering, interrupts, nudges, cron wakeups, idle lifecycle — and an inner loop that owns a turn — one LLM call plus one tool batch, repeated under an iteration budget until the model produces plain text. This page walks both in algorithmic detail, with the actual loop bodies and the ordering invariants that make them safe."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"The loop stack","a":"loop-stack","x":"The loop stack There are really four nested control layers, plus a retry sub-loop: Layer Owner Unit of work Where L0 — transport/wakeup platform adapters, cron ticker, /loop , goal loop one inbound event gateway/platforms/base.py:5981 , cron/scheduler_provider.py:505 L1 — session serialization adapter guard + turn-lease registry one session gateway/platforms/base.py:5818 , gateway/turn_lease.py:152 L2 — outer loop (turn chain) _run_agent_inner recursive drain one user turn + queued follow-ups gateway/run.py:27763 L3 — inner loop (tool loop) run_conversation one LLM call + tool batch agent/conversation_loop.py:1922 L3.5 — retry sub-loop while retry_count < max_retries one API attempt agent/conversation_loop.py:2741 L4 — tool batch execution segmented parallel/sequential executor one tool call agent/tool_executor.py:1048"},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop: intake and the three-level busy guard","a":"outer-intake","x":"Outer loop: intake and the three-level busy guard Every inbound message crosses three gates before it can start a turn: Adapter guard (synchronous). If the session already has an entry in _active_sessions , the message goes to the busy handler instead of the runner ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6151 ). Photo bursts merge, text may debounce, otherwise the message lands in the per-session pending slot ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6182 ). For an idle session the guard is installed before the task spawn to close the race — the code cites grammY's sequentialize pattern ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6190 ). Runner busy handler. _handle_active_session_busy_message ( gateway/run.py:9945 ) decides steer vs redirect vs interrupt vs queue (next section). Turn lease. SessionTurnLeaseRegistry holds an asyncio.Lock per resolved session_id — not routing key — so two routing keys mapped to one session serialize; timeout raises and the caller must not enter the load/run/flush region: fail closed ( gateway/turn_lease.py:191 ). Queueing is a single head slot with album-merge semantics plus an overflow FIFO behind it ( gateway/run.py:9852 ). The historical bug is documented inline: the old overwrite path silently mashed two user messages into one turn ( gateway/run.py:9853 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop: steer / redirect / interrupt","a":"outer-steering","x":"Outer loop: steer / redirect / interrupt The busy handler resolves an effective mode from display.busy_input_mode ∈ {interrupt, queue, steer} (default interrupt , gateway/run.py:9369 ) and then applies two safety demotions : interrupt is demoted to queue when subagents are active — an interrupt cascades into children and would destroy minutes of work — and when compression is in flight ( gateway/run.py:10112 , gateway/run.py:10123 ). Verb Semantics Where steer(text) Appends guidance to the last tool result at the next iteration boundary. Never kills anything. run_agent.py:3381 , applied at gateway/run.py:10162 redirect(text) Aborts only the in-flight model request (never a running tool — it degrades to steer while tools execute, run_agent.py:3457 ) and re-anchors the turn on the new text. run_agent.py:3417 , applied at gateway/run.py:10180 interrupt(text) Kills the model request and fans the interrupt out to all tool worker threads and children. run_agent.py:3180 The queue-unless-consumed invariant : if not steered and not redirected: queue it — a successful steer must never also be replayed as a next-turn user message ( gateway/run.py:10200 ). A separate 200 ms poller inside the run task peeks (never pops) pending interrupts, so a message is never lost if the agent finishes first ( gateway/run.py:28437 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop proper: the recursive turn chain","a":"outer-chain","x":"Outer loop proper: the recursive turn chain The outer loop is tail recursion bounded by depth , not a while . After a turn returns, the runner drains follow-ups in priority order — pending queue, then result[\"interrupt_message\"] (unless it is a control interrupt like \"Stop requested\", gateway/run.py:3356 ), then leftover /steer text — and recurses with _interrupt_depth + 1 , capped at _MAX_INTERRUPT_DEPTH = 3 ( gateway/run.py:25773 , recursion at gateway/run.py:29307 ). There is a second, adapter-level drain for follow-ups that land after the runner returned: it spawns a fresh task rather than awaiting recursively, because the old await chain grew one C-stack frame per follow-up and segfaulted around 2000 ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6765 ). Crucially it .clear() s the guard Event but never deletes the _active_sessions entry — deleting would admit a second concurrent agent on the same session key ( gateway/platforms/base.py:6768 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop: nudges (the learning-loop trigger)","a":"outer-nudges","x":"Outer loop: nudges (the learning-loop trigger) Two counters drive the self-improvement machinery (detailed on Self-Adaptive Skills ): Memory nudge — turn-counted. _turns_since_memory increments per user turn; at memory.nudge_interval (default 10) it arms should_review_memory ( agent/turn_context.py:704 ). The counter is hydrated from persisted history on session resume so a restart doesn't reset the clock ( agent/turn_context.py:655 ). Skill nudge — iteration-counted. _iters_since_skill increments once per API call inside the inner loop ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1989 ) and is evaluated at turn end ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:771 ). Both counters reset whenever the corresponding tool is actually used ( agent/tool_executor.py:667 ). When either fires, the finalizer forks a background review agent after delivery ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:791 ) — a daemon thread with tools restricted to memory + skills. A still-running review from the previous turn is cancelled at the top of the next turn so two forks never make concurrent API calls on the same credentials ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1780 ). Nudges are not prompt injections. The turn prologue explicitly preserves the original user message ( agent/turn_context.py:700 ); the nudge is a boolean flag that spawns an out-of-band fork. The live conversation never sees it."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop: scheduled cron wakeups","a":"outer-cron","x":"Outer loop: scheduled cron wakeups The ticker is a daemon-thread loop inside the gateway process ( cron/scheduler_provider.py:559 ) with a 60 s interval, exponential backoff on consecutive failures, and — deliberately — except BaseException , so a SystemExit escaping a provider SDK cannot silently kill scheduling. Each tick() ( cron/scheduler.py:6730 ): reap dead-owner executions → collect due jobs → sweep stale in-flight claims → advance next_run_at first, under the file lock (at-most-once semantics, cron/scheduler.py:6901 ) → partition workdir-mutating jobs onto a sequential pool → claim at execution start, not submit time ( cron/scheduler.py:6935 ) → run. A cron turn runs run_conversation on a single-worker pool with a copied contextvar context so the ticker thread can watchdog it on idle seconds — not wall clock — and hard-interrupt on inactivity ( cron/scheduler.py:5639 ). Cron sets skip_background_review=True : review forks cost ~30K tokens per event and cron has no human in the loop ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:788 ). A global ESTOP file pauses all new dispatch without killing in-flight work ( agent/estop.py:1 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Outer loop: idle behavior & session lifecycle","a":"outer-idle","x":"Outer loop: idle behavior & session lifecycle Per-turn inactivity watchdog — a thread (so it survives event-loop starvation) polling seconds_since_activity ; timeout default 1800 s of idle time, so a busy turn can legally run for hours ( gateway/run.py:3320 ). Activity is stamped at every meaningful point: API call start, stream chunk received, tool start, tool results posted, backoff heartbeats ( run_agent.py:3918 ). Session reset/expiry — mode ∈ {none, idle, daily, both} ; both paths refuse to expire a session that still has live background processes ( gateway/session.py:2424 , gateway/session.py:2326 ). Scale-to-zero — when idle (no in-flight turn, no inbound, no background work), the gateway goes dormant and self-suspends via the Fly Machines socket, because the Fly proxy judges idleness on inbound connections only and cannot see an in-flight turn ( gateway/scale_to_zero.py:1 ). Wakeups deliver a synthetic internal MessageEvent , or a self-POST for stateless adapters ( gateway/wake.py:1 ). Other outer loops — /loop recurring in-session wakeups that fire only while the session is idle ( hermes_cli/loops.py:1 ), and the Ralph-style kanban goal loop ( hermes_cli/goals.py:2189 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: header, budget, and steer drain","a":"inner-header","x":"Inner loop: header, budget, and steer drain Entry is run_conversation at agent/conversation_loop.py:1704 . The prologue ( build_turn_context , agent/turn_context.py:431 ) does everything once-per-turn: sanitize, hydrate nudge counters, restore-or-build the system prompt, run preflight compression, fire the pre_llm_call hook, prefetch external memory, and allocate a fresh IterationBudget . Then the loop head, verbatim: while (api_call_count < agent.max_iterations and agent.iteration_budget.remaining > 0) or agent._budget_grace_call: _redirect_text = agent._drain_pending_redirect() ... agent._checkpoint_mgr.new_turn() if agent._interrupt_requested: interrupted = True; break api_call_count += 1 agent._touch_activity(f\"starting API call #{api_call_count}\") ...budget consume or break... ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1922 .) The budget ( agent/iteration_budget.py:17 ) is a lock-guarded counter with consume() / refund() ; the gateway raises max_iterations to 500 ( gateway/run.py:2042 ), and each subagent gets an independent budget (default 50), so total iterations across a delegation tree can exceed the parent cap. A steer that arrived during the previous API call is drained before request assembly and appended to the last role:\"tool\" message so the model sees it on this iteration; on a first iteration with no tool message, it is put back rather than breaking role alternation ( agent/c"},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: request assembly","a":"inner-request","x":"Inner loop: request assembly Sanitize tool-call arguments with a prefix cursor so already-validated history isn't re-parsed every iteration ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2062 ). Drop legacy ghost interrupt-scaffold rows ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2081 ). Repair role alternation ( tool → user , user → user tails) and recompute the flush cursor ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2109 ). Build api_messages by structural clone — never msg.copy() — so in-place transforms can't corrupt the durable transcript ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2118 ). Pre-API compression gate (below), then per-provider prompt-cache re-decoration and _build_api_kwargs ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2807 ), then a whole-payload surrogate sanitization chokepoint ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2832 )."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: retry sub-loop and the API call","a":"inner-retry","x":"Inner loop: retry sub-loop and the API call Each iteration runs while retry_count < max_retries (default 3, agent/conversation_loop.py:2741 ) with a fresh TurnRetryState holding ~16 one-shot recovery guards plus four restart signals ( agent/turn_retry_state.py:33 ). Streaming is the default; the call is dispatched through middleware to either the interruptible streaming transport or relay_llm.execute ( agent/conversation_loop.py:3019 ). The trickiest ordering guarantee is the redirect-crossing window : _model_request_active is set and cleared under the same lock redirect() observes, so a response and a redirect that cross on different threads cause the now-stale response to be discarded , never silently delivered ( agent/conversation_loop.py:3056 ). Inside a stream, the chunk loop touches activity per chunk, checks the interrupt flag, and closes the stream on the owning thread — abandoning a half-read SSE response would permanently leak the pooled connection ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:4038 ). Backoff sleeps in 200 ms slices so interrupts land immediately and 30 s activity touches keep the watchdog fed ( agent/conversation_loop.py:6415 ). After the retry loop, every restart signal — redirected, compressed, rebuilt-on-fallback, length-continuation — rewinds api_call_count and refunds the budget, so a rebuilt request never costs an iteration ( agent/conversation_loop.py:64"},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: compaction triggers & token accounting","a":"inner-compaction","x":"Inner loop: compaction triggers & token accounting Post-response, usage is normalized into canonical buckets and fed to context_compressor.update_from_response ( agent/conversation_loop.py:4028 ). Three compaction trigger sites share one compression_attempts counter (cap 3): Turn-prologue preflight — with a \"defer to real usage\" guard so a noisy rough estimate doesn't trigger compaction the provider's own prompt count would refute ( agent/turn_context.py:860 ). Pre-API mid-turn preflight — because a single turn can balloon on tool results while the post-response gate only sees lagging counts ( agent/conversation_loop.py:2557 ). A lock-skip refunds the attempt: a defer is not evidence about compressibility. Post-tool-batch gate — on real prompt tokens only (reasoning tokens inflate completion counts and caused premature compaction), with a sentinel for \"compaction just ran, no reading yet\" and a schema-inclusive rough estimate as last resort ( agent/conversation_loop.py:7305 ). When over threshold but blocked, a deterministic LLM-free prune of old tool results runs instead ( agent/context_compressor.py:3691 ). The compression budget rearms only on provider-confirmed recovery — a real prompt count below threshold on the request immediately after compaction ( agent/conversation_loop.py:4021 ). The decision threshold is (context_length − max_tokens) × threshold_percent (default 0.5"},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: response handling","a":"inner-response","x":"Inner loop: response handling The branch is simply if assistant_message.tool_calls: ( agent/conversation_loop.py:6820 ). Tool-call path runs a validation ladder — duplicate-id uniquify, fuzzy name repair, mixed-batch partial erroring, 3-strike all-invalid handling, JSON arg validation, truncation detection, invalid-JSON recovery injected as tool-role results (never user messages: role alternation) — then the two invariants: the assistant message is appended and flushed to SQLite before any tool executes (a destructive tool can restart Hermes; resume logic must see the exact tool-call block that ran, agent/conversation_loop.py:7161 ), and a flush failure aborts the turn rather than executing from process-local state. Batches consisting only of execute_code refund their iteration ( agent/conversation_loop.py:7288 ). Final-response path is an ordered ladder of continuation gates, each able to set final_response = None and continue: codex intermediate-ack continuation (cap 2), length-continuation joining, dropped-tool-call re-prompt (cap 3, agent/conversation_loop.py:7926 ), scaffolding strip, verify-on-stop nudge, pre_verify hook nudge, kanban terminal-tool nudge — then terminate ( agent/conversation_loop.py:8156 ). An empty-response recovery ladder runs first: partial-stream salvage → cached housekeeping content → post-tool nudge → thinking prefill → bounded empty retries with a "},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Inner loop: finalization","a":"inner-finalize","x":"Inner loop: finalization finalize_turn ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:120 ): if a verification gate withheld an answer and the budget then ran out, that exact answer is reused rather than paying for another fallible call; otherwise budget exhaustion triggers one extra toolless API call asking for a summary ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:192 ). Then: persist, return leftover steer as pending_steer , clear interrupts, evaluate the skill nudge, sync external memory, spawn the background review, fire on_session_end , and return the result dict (final_response, exit reason, token buckets, cost, flags)."},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"Tool execution layer","a":"tool-layer","x":"Tool execution layer Dispatch selection ( run_agent.py:8170 ): a single call runs sequential; a batch is planned into segments — maximal contiguous parallel-safe runs separated by sequential barriers, preserving the model's emission order exactly ( agent/tool_dispatch_helpers.py:117 ). Path-scoped tools carry reader/writer reservations: reader↔reader overlap commutes, any writer overlap closes the run; search_files reserves its root as a reader so a search after a write into that subtree is ordered behind it. Concurrent executor ( agent/tool_executor.py:1048 ): up to 8 workers on a DaemonThreadPoolExecutor — daemon because a wedged non-daemon worker would block interpreter exit forever ( agent/tool_executor.py:1458 ). A start-order gate serializes dispatch by submit order even though execution is parallel, so approval prompts appear in order; on gate timeout it proceeds out of order rather than starving ( agent/tool_executor.py:1200 ). Deadlines : 420 s batch default, with human-approval wait time excluded from the deadline ( agent/tool_executor.py:1526 ). On abandon: shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True) — wedged workers are left detached rather than deadlocking the batch ( agent/tool_executor.py:1622 ). Results : pre-allocated slots appended in original call order — wall-clock completion order never leaks into the transcript ( agent/tool_executor.py:1172 ). Every tool_cal"},{"u":"loops.html","p":"Turn Loops","t":"The invariants (condensed)","a":"invariants","x":"The invariants (condensed) # Invariant Where 1 One turn per session, enforced three times (adapter guard, busy handler, turn lease — fail closed). gateway/turn_lease.py:191 2 Clear the guard Event on drain, never delete the entry — deleting admits a second concurrent agent. gateway/platforms/base.py:6768 3 Canonical persistence precedes side effects: the assistant tool-call row is flushed before tools run; a flush failure aborts the turn. agent/conversation_loop.py:7161 4 Every tool_call_id gets exactly one result across all exit paths. agent/conversation_loop.py:8227 5 A transcript must never end on a tool row — strict providers hallucinate continuations otherwise. agent/message_sanitization.py:296 6 Rebuild-and-retry never costs an iteration: rewind + refund on every restart signal; execute_code batches refunded. agent/conversation_loop.py:6452 7 A response that crossed a redirect is discarded, not delivered. agent/conversation_loop.py:3094 8 Never serialize raw chain-of-thought into replayable content — a poisoned checkpoint reads as a prefill jailbreak to provider classifiers and bricks the session with deterministic empty-response storms. agent/conversation_loop.py:268 9 Timeouts are idle-based, not wall-clock; long busy turns are legal. gateway/run.py:3320 10 Cron advances next_run_at under the file lock before executing — at-most-once semantics. cron/scheduler.py:6901 11"},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"LLM Kernels & Adapters","a":"","x":"The \"kernel\" of the agentic loop is the layer where inference, tool-calling and streaming actually execute — per provider dialect. Hermes deliberately has no single LLM-client class: it splits the job into declarative provider profiles, format-converting transports, an imperative client-lifecycle + streaming layer, and a relay wrapper around every physical attempt. This page documents each tier, each dialect's exact conversion and streaming mechanics, and where the loop invokes them."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"The four-tier shape","a":"shape","x":"The four-tier shape Tier Owns Where Provider profile (declarative) per-provider request quirks, auth metadata, catalog providers/base.py:39 , 36 bundled profiles in plugins/model-providers/*/ Transport (format conversion) messages/tools dialect conversion + response normalization agent/transports/* + the fat adapters agent/*_adapter.py Client lifecycle + streaming (imperative) SDK client construction/caching/abort, streaming loops, delta assembly run_agent.py:5311 , agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:3833 , agent/codex_runtime.py:1038 Relay wrapper (observability/interception) wraps every physical attempt in NeMo Relay spans/codecs agent/relay_llm.py:32 The abstraction boundary is stated in the transport ABC's own docstring: \"A transport owns the data path for one api_mode: convert_messages → convert_tools → build_kwargs → normalize_response. It does NOT own: client construction, streaming, credential refresh, prompt caching, interrupt handling, or retry logic. Those stay on AIAgent.\" ( agent/transports/base.py:1 ). Four transports register: ChatCompletionsTransport ( agent/transports/chat_completions.py:234 ), AnthropicTransport ( agent/transports/anthropic.py:13 ), ResponsesApiTransport ( agent/transports/codex.py:287 ), BedrockTransport ( agent/transports/bedrock.py:15 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Which SDKs are wrapped","a":"sdks","x":"Which SDKs are wrapped openai==2.24.0 — core dependency ( pyproject.toml:40 ), used for both chat.completions and responses . anthropic==0.87.0 — optional [anthropic] extra, lazily imported ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:56 ). boto3==1.42.89 — optional [bedrock] extra ( agent/bedrock_adapter.py:64 ). httpx[socks]==0.28.1 — used raw for the hand-rolled Gemini native client and for custom http-client injection (keepalives, Entra bearer hooks). google-auth — token minting only for Vertex, never inference ( agent/vertex_adapter.py:36 ). There is no Google GenAI SDK anywhere. nemo-relay — the observability wrapper ( agent/relay_runtime.py:1609 ). Beyond the real SDKs, a family of duck-typed \"fake OpenAI clients\" all expose .chat.completions.create(**kwargs) so ~45 loop call sites never branch: GeminiNativeClient ( agent/gemini_native_adapter.py:1045 ), CopilotACPClient ( agent/copilot_acp_client.py:450 ), MoAClient ( agent/moa_loop.py:2313 ), and the auxiliary Codex/Anthropic/Bedrock shims in agent/auxiliary_client.py:1902 ff. Even ToolCall duck-types the OpenAI SDK via @property function → self ( agent/transports/types.py:45 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Dialect: chat_completions (OpenAI-compatible)","a":"chat","x":"Dialect: chat_completions (OpenAI-compatible) Message conversion is a security/compat boundary, not a format converter : convert_messages() ( agent/transports/chat_completions.py:244 ) deletes Hermes-internal and foreign-dialect keys strict providers 400 on — codex_reasoning_items , tool_name , api_content , timestamps, any _ -prefixed key — keeping Gemini's extra_content thought signatures only for Gemini-family models ( agent/transports/chat_completions.py:218 ). System prompt stays at messages[0] ; the role swaps system → developer for GPT-5/Codex families ( agent/transports/chat_completions.py:501 ). Request quirks come from three profile hooks — prepare_messages() , build_extra_body() , build_api_kwargs_extras() ( providers/base.py:133 ); a legacy ~20-boolean flag path survives only for unregistered providers ( agent/transports/chat_completions.py:498 ). Streaming : chat.completions.create(stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True}) , deltas assembled in _call_chat_completions ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:3833 ). Tool-call accumulation: name is assigned , arguments are concatenated — MiniMax resends full names, Ollama reuses indices ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:4220 ). After the stream: per-call JSON validity check, argument repair, and distinct handling of \"truncated by length\" vs \"stream dropped mid-tool-call\". The streamed result is repackaged into "},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Dialect: anthropic_messages","a":"anthropic","x":"Dialect: anthropic_messages Conversion : convert_messages_to_anthropic() ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:2785 ) hoists system messages out into the top-level system , then runs six post-passes: strip orphaned tool blocks → merge consecutive roles → ensure a leading user turn → manage thinking signatures → evict old screenshots → scrub blank text blocks. Tools : function.parameters → input_schema , with nullable-union stripping ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1789 ). Client : build_anthropic_client() auth-branches per endpoint kind — API key, bearer, or the Claude-Code OAuth identity described on Providers & Tiers — always with max_retries=0 and a 900 s timeout ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:793 ). Streaming : client.messages.stream() as a context manager; Hermes iterates SDK events itself ( content_block_start/delta , thinking deltas) and takes the final message from get_final_message() ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:4464 ). A parallel AnthropicStreamAccumulator rebuilds the message from raw SSE — including input_json_delta partial-JSON accumulation — when the relay modified the output ( agent/relay_llm.py:810 ). Parse-back : content blocks split into text / thinking / tool_use, stop reasons mapped ( tool_use→tool_calls , max_tokens→length , …), and the OAuth mcp__ tool-name prefix reversed by registry lookup ( agent/transports/anthropic.py:80 ). Interleaved thinking ordering. When"},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Dialect: codex_responses (OpenAI Responses API)","a":"codex","x":"Dialect: codex_responses (OpenAI Responses API) Conversion : system messages become the top-level instructions ; assistant tool calls become function_call items and tool results become function_call_output items — with multimodal parts allowed in outputs ( agent/codex_responses_adapter.py:410 ). Tools flatten to {type:\"function\", name, description, strict:false, parameters} ( agent/codex_responses_adapter.py:341 ). Streaming is deliberately low-level : Hermes calls responses.create(stream=True) raw instead of the SDK's high-level responses.stream() , because the consumer Codex backend drifts response.completed.response.output to null and the SDK's reconstruction throws mid-iteration. The final object is assembled only from response.output_item.done events ( agent/codex_runtime.py:1349 , rationale at agent/codex_runtime.py:955 ; event consumer at agent/codex_runtime.py:1038 ). Reasoning continuity : reasoning items with encrypted_content are captured and replayed as input items, guarded by a per-item issuer filter and a session kill switch, so encrypted reasoning never replays cross-endpoint ( agent/codex_responses_adapter.py:1458 ). Always store: false ; tools omitted entirely when empty (the SDK iterates it without a None guard); Codex session headers and xAI conversation ids stamped per request ( agent/transports/codex.py:337 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Dialect: Gemini native (raw httpx, no SDK)","a":"gemini","x":"Dialect: Gemini native (raw httpx, no SDK) When the base URL is native generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta (not the /openai subpath), create_openai_client returns GeminiNativeClient — a raw-httpx client masquerading as the OpenAI SDK ( agent/agent_runtime_helpers.py:2497 , agent/gemini_native_adapter.py:1045 ). System messages become a separate systemInstruction ; roles map assistant→model , tool results become functionResponse parts; alternation repair refuses to fuse human text into a functionResponse turn, interposing a placeholder model turn instead ( agent/gemini_native_adapter.py:450 ). Tools become functionDeclarations with schema sanitization ( agent/gemini_schema.py:134 ); tool_choice maps to functionCallingConfig.mode AUTO/ANY/NONE. Streaming is hand-rolled SSE against :streamGenerateContent?alt=sse . Gemini re-sends the whole args object each event, so Hermes diffs and emits only the incremental suffix ( agent/gemini_native_adapter.py:889 ). Gemini-3 thoughtSignature s round-trip via extra_content , with a literal \"skip_thought_signature_validator\" sentinel for cross-provider replays ( agent/gemini_native_adapter.py:353 ). Vertex AI is not a separate dialect — agent/vertex_adapter.py only mints a bearer token and builds the OpenAI-compatible Vertex URL ( agent/vertex_adapter.py:190 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Dialect: bedrock_converse","a":"bedrock","x":"Dialect: bedrock_converse Pure boto3 ( agent/transports/bedrock.py:1 ): tools become toolSpec.inputSchema.json ( agent/bedrock_adapter.py:488 ); streaming iterates the boto3 event stream, accumulating tool-argument JSON as a string and parsing at contentBlockStop ( agent/bedrock_adapter.py:845 ). The preferred path for Claude-on-Bedrock is not Converse at all — it is anthropic.AnthropicBedrock , for full parity on prompt caching, adaptive thinking and the 1M-context beta ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:949 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Where the loop invokes the kernels","a":"callsites","x":"Where the loop invokes the kernels Per inner-loop iteration: build_api_kwargs() ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:1825 ) → cache markers re-applied per provider per retry attempt → _perform_api_call() ( agent/conversation_loop.py:3019 ) — streaming-preferred via _interruptible_streaming_api_call , else relay_llm.execute — wrapped in LLM-execution middleware → transport.validate_response() → transport.normalize_response() → build_assistant_message() . The main loop is fully synchronous. Requests run on a daemon worker thread the caller polls for interrupts and staleness; cron and delegated-child contexts run inline instead, to avoid nested-thread-pool deadlocks ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:1002 ). Async exists only in the auxiliary layer, as asyncio.to_thread wrappers. Client lifecycle safety. Per-request clients exist because of a real memory-corruption class: a watchdog thread must never close() a client whose TLS socket another thread is reading. Stranger threads only shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) the socket; owner threads do the SDK close; both OpenAI and Anthropic request clients sit in a single-slot warm cache with in_use / poisoned flags ( run_agent.py:5429 , run_agent.py:5344 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Retries, timeouts, rate limits, fallback","a":"retries","x":"Retries, timeouts, rate limits, fallback SDK retries are off everywhere ( max_retries=0 ) — the SDK default ignores Retry-After and double-retries inside Hermes's own loop ( run_agent.py:5331 , agent/anthropic_adapter.py:858 ). All retry/backoff/rotation/fallback lives in the outer retry loop. Three retry layers : SDK (disabled) → stream-level ( HERMES_STREAM_RETRIES , default 2) → turn-level with jittered backoff (5–120 s), Retry-After parsing, and provider-specific ladders ( agent/retry_utils.py:90 ). Timeouts : per-provider request_timeout_seconds ; streaming read timeout auto-raised for local endpoints (vLLM/Ollama prefill can take minutes) and reasoning models; stale-stream watchdogs with per-family floors and a cross-turn stale-streak circuit breaker ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:3836 ). Rate limits : headers captured on every response ( run_agent.py:4043 ); account-level 429 distinguished from upstream-capacity 429 by inspecting the 429's own headers; credential-pool rotation runs before model fallback ( run_agent.py:6423 ). Fallback chain : try_activate_fallback() arms an exponential primary cooldown, walks the chain skipping identical backends, and rebuilds the client through the same central router the auxiliary layer uses ( agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:2427 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Prompt caching","a":"caching","x":"Prompt caching Anthropic-style breakpoints : a 4-marker budget — static system prefix, whole system, then the last N cacheable messages — planned by build_prompt_cache_plan() ( agent/prompt_caching.py:385 ). Native layout puts markers on inner content blocks; OpenAI-wire proxies get envelope markers. Re-decorated per retry attempt so a mid-turn failover re-plans for the new provider ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1490 ). prompt_cache_key (OpenAI/xAI/Codex routing hint): one hash — pck_<sha256[:24]> over scope + instructions + sorted tools — with cron's per-fire timestamp stripped from the scope and a rotation-stable logical scope resolved from the compression lineage root ( agent/transports/codex.py:143 , agent/prompt_cache_scope.py:67 ). Sticky routing keys : OpenRouter and Nous put session_id in the body; Codex mirrors it into x-client-request-id , xAI into x-grok-conv-id ( agent/transports/codex.py:627 )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Reasoning / thinking parameters","a":"reasoning","x":"Reasoning / thinking parameters Hermes's canonical effort ladder is none | minimal | low | medium | high | xhigh | max | ultra ( hermes_cli/models.py:1711 ), translated per dialect: Dialect Wire form Where Anthropic ≥4.6 (adaptive) thinking={\"type\":\"adaptive\"} + output_config.effort agent/anthropic_adapter.py:3055 Anthropic legacy thinking={\"type\":\"enabled\",\"budget_tokens\":N} , forced temperature=1 agent/anthropic_adapter.py:3072 Responses / Codex reasoning={\"effort\", \"summary\":\"auto\"} + include=[\"reasoning.encrypted_content\"] agent/transports/codex.py:426 xAI Responses reasoning.effort only when the model supports it; per-model clamping agent/transports/codex.py:439 OpenRouter / generic chat extra_body.reasoning , clamped to the catalog's supported_efforts agent/transports/chat_completions.py:635 Kimi/Moonshot top-level reasoning_effort + extra_body.thinking agent/transports/chat_completions.py:554 Gemini thinkingConfig {budget, includeThoughts, level}; omitted for Gemma (400s); output cap raised because thoughts bill against it agent/transports/chat_completions.py:100 On the chat wire, providers that drop summary-part boundaries get them re-inserted by separate_glued_reasoning_blocks() ( agent/reasoning_summaries.py:37 ); DeepSeek-v4/Kimi thinking modes require reasoning_content on every assistant tool-call message, so it is padded with a single space when absent ( agent/chat"},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Special kernels","a":"special","x":"Special kernels Kernel What it is Where codex_app_server Opt-in: Hermes hands the whole turn to a codex app-server subprocess over newline-delimited JSON-RPC, consuming item/* notifications until turn/completed . Hermes tools are re-exposed to the subprocess as a stdio MCP server — deliberately excluding terminal, file tools and the agent-loop tools that need live state. agent/transports/codex_app_server.py:53 , agent/transports/hermes_tools_mcp_server.py:1 MoA A virtual provider with no wire of its own: reference models fan out in a thread pool, a guidance block is built, and the aggregator slot's real client answers. agent/moa_loop.py:2313 Copilot ACP Shells out to copilot --acp over stdio; no true streaming (one-shot faked into two chunks); tool calls recovered from <tool_call>{…}</tool_call> text by regex. agent/copilot_acp_client.py:450 , regex at agent/copilot_acp_client.py:36 Local models No dedicated vLLM/Ollama transport — plain chat_completions with accommodations: raised read timeouts for prefill, Ollama index-reuse workaround, num_ctx passthrough, llama.cpp grammar-parse recovery (strip pattern / format and retry once). agent/chat_completion_helpers.py:3850 , agent/conversation_loop.py:4956 Auxiliary layer Compression, titles, vision, MoA advisors, subagent helpers all route through one central router whose contract is \"the returned client always exposes .chat.compl"},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Tool schemas: from registry to every dialect","a":"schemas","x":"Tool schemas: from registry to every dialect Schemas are hand-written dicts , self-registered at import time — there is no signature introspection anywhere in this path: registry.register(name=\"read_file\", toolset=\"file\", schema=READ_FILE_SCHEMA, handler=_handle_read_file, check_fn=_check_file_reqs, ...) ( tools/file_tools.py:2809 .) The pipeline: toolsets.py composes toolsets ( toolsets.py:107 ) → get_tool_definitions() memoizes on registry generation + config mtime ( model_tools.py:323 ) → ToolRegistry.get_definitions() filters by check_fn and applies dynamic_schema_overrides — which is how delegate_task 's description reflects live concurrency config ( tools/registry.py:1018 ) → a cross-provider hardening pass renames illegal property keys, strips $ref siblings and top-level combinators, and collapses nullable unions ( tools/schema_sanitizer.py:120 ), with the inverse rename applied at dispatch so handlers see original names ( model_tools.py:831 ). Each transport then converts that one canonical list to its dialect (identity, input_schema , flat Responses shape, toolSpec.inputSchema.json , functionDeclarations )."},{"u":"kernels.html","p":"LLM Kernels","t":"Summary matrix","a":"matrix","x":"Summary matrix Dialect SDK / wire Streaming Tool calls on the wire Parse-back chat_completions openai==2.24.0 SDK SSE, deltas assembled by hand tools[].function delta.tool_calls : name assigned, args concatenated anthropic_messages anthropic==0.87.0 messages.stream() + parallel raw-SSE accumulator input_schema tool_use blocks; input_json_delta partial JSON codex_responses openai Responses resource raw stream=True event iterator (deliberately not responses.stream() ) flat {type:\"function\",...} function_call output items bedrock_converse boto3 boto3 event stream toolSpec.inputSchema.json toolUse deltas, JSON parsed at block stop Gemini native raw httpx hand-rolled SSE functionDeclarations parts[].functionCall , args suffix-diffed copilot-acp subprocess JSON-RPC none (faked chunks) described in prompt text XML <tool_call> regex codex_app_server subprocess JSON-RPC 2.0 item/* notifications codex-owned; Hermes tools via stdio MCP event projector moa (virtual) none of its own delegates to aggregator inherited inherited"},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Providers & Subscription Tiers","a":"","x":"Hermes speaks to ~40 inference providers through four API dialects. The interesting part is the \"subscription contracts\" — consumer subscriptions (Claude Pro/Max, ChatGPT Plus/Pro, SuperGrok, GitHub Copilot, Qwen, MiniMax Coding Plan) reused as agent inference, each requiring its own OAuth flow, token store, refresh logic, and — for some — client-identity spoofing to pass the vendor's edge. This page documents each one, one by one, grounded in the auth code."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"How a provider is defined — four joined layers","a":"layers","x":"How a provider is defined — four joined layers Layer Owns Where ProviderProfile (declarative plugin) api_mode, base_url, env_vars, auth_type, default_headers, fallback models, request quirks providers/base.py:39 Plugin discovery imports plugins/model-providers/<name>/ , then $HERMES_HOME plugins, then legacy modules providers/__init__.py:271 ProviderConfig registry the credential truth: auth_type, key env vars, base-url env var, OAuth client_id/scope hermes_cli/auth.py:232 CANONICAL_PROVIDERS the slug universe shown by hermes model hermes_cli/models.py:1155 Unification happens in provider_catalog.py ( hermes_cli/provider_catalog.py:83 ), which tags each slug with a desktop tab derived purely from its auth type. The four API dialects ( api_mode ): chat_completions (OpenAI wire), anthropic_messages , codex_responses (OpenAI Responses API), bedrock_converse . See LLM Kernels for how each dialect actually executes."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Config & credential file locations","a":"files","x":"Config & credential file locations Path Contents ~/.hermes/config.yaml model.provider/default/base_url/api_mode , auxiliary.* , custom_providers[] , credential_pool_strategies ( hermes_cli/auth.py:7387 ) ~/.hermes/.env provider API keys — the canonical secret store ( hermes_cli/credential_lifecycle.py:1 ) ~/.hermes/auth.json providers.<id> OAuth singletons + credential_pool.<id>[] + active_provider ( hermes_cli/auth.py:1047 ) ~/.claude/.credentials.json + macOS Keychain Claude Code OAuth — read and written back by Hermes ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1046 ) ~/.codex/auth.json Codex CLI tokens — read-only for Hermes ( hermes_cli/auth.py:4052 ) ~/.qwen/oauth_creds.json Qwen CLI OAuth — read and written back ( hermes_cli/auth.py:2664 ) ~/.hermes/.copilot_jwt.json exchanged Copilot JWT cache ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:328 )"},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"The credential pool (multi-account rotation)","a":"pool","x":"The credential pool (multi-account rotation) Every provider gets a pool keyed by slug ( agent/credential_pool.py:164 ), auth types oauth / api_key , sources like manual , env:<VAR> , claude_code , hermes_pkce , device_code , gh_cli , qwen-cli . Rotation strategies are fill_first | round_robin | random | least_used . Per-provider refresh dispatch lives in _refresh_entry_impl ( agent/credential_pool.py:1365 ); Codex and xAI refreshes are wrapped in a cross-process auth.json flock because their refresh tokens are single-use ( agent/credential_pool.py:1313 )."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"All providers at a glance","a":"table","x":"All providers at a glance ~40 providers across the four dialects and six auth types. The subscription-tier ones — where a consumer plan drives agent inference — are marked ★ . Slug Dialect Auth Tier? nous — Nous Portal chat_completions oauth_device_code ★ Nous plan anthropic anthropic_messages api_key + OAuth ★ Claude Pro/Max openai-codex codex_responses oauth_external ★ ChatGPT Plus/Pro openai-api codex_responses api_key — xai-oauth codex_responses oauth_external ★ SuperGrok / X Premium+ xai codex_responses api_key — copilot per-model copilot (GitHub OAuth→JWT) ★ Copilot Ind/Bus/Ent copilot-acp chat_completions (subprocess) external_process ★ via copilot CLI qwen-oauth chat_completions oauth_external ★ Qwen Portal tier minimax-oauth anthropic_messages oauth_minimax ★ MiniMax Coding Plan zai — GLM chat_completions api_key partly — GLM Coding Plan kimi-coding chat_completions api_key partly — Kimi Coding Plan alibaba-coding-plan chat_completions api_key partly — Coding Plan stepfun chat_completions api_key partly — Step Plan opencode-go per-model api_key partly — $10/mo key gemini , vertex , bedrock , azure-foundry varies api_key / GCP / AWS / Entra — …plus fireworks , openrouter , novita , lmstudio , deepseek , huggingface , nvidia , ollama-cloud , deepinfra , ai-gateway , kilocode , arcee , gmi , xiaomi , meta-ai , upstage , custom … (all plain api_key) Terms of service. Subsc"},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Claude Pro / Max — the deepest subscription integration","a":"anthropic","x":"Claude Pro / Max — the deepest subscription integration Slug anthropic , base https://api.anthropic.com , anthropic_messages dialect. A token's prefix decides its auth: sk-ant-api* → API key ( x-api-key ); sk-ant-* /JWT/ cc-* → OAuth (Bearer) ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:414 ). Resolution priority runs env vars → the Claude Code credential store (Keychain / ~/.claude/.credentials.json , refreshed if expired) → the credential pool ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1391 ). OAuth client & refresh ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1496 ): client_id = \"9d1c250a-e61b-44d9-88ed-5944d1962f5e\" _OAUTH_TOKEN_URLS = [\"https://platform.claude.com/v1/oauth/token\", \"https://console.anthropic.com/v1/oauth/token\"] _OAUTH_TOKEN_USER_AGENT = \"axios/1.7.9\" # deliberate spoof The axios/1.7.9 UA is deliberate and documented: Anthropic rate-limits (429) any token-endpoint request whose UA starts with claude-code/ , and the real CLI exchanges the code with a bare axios client ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1506 ). Hermes writes refreshed tokens back to ~/.claude/.credentials.json atomically at mode 0600, preserving scopes because Claude Code ≥2.1.81 gates on the presence of user:inference ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:1246 ). How Hermes presents itself as Claude Code at runtime ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:908 ): user-agent: claude-code/<detected-cli-version> (external, cli) , x-app: cli , the OAuth-only beta head"},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"ChatGPT / Codex subscription ( openai-codex )","a":"codex","x":"ChatGPT / Codex subscription ( openai-codex ) Base https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/codex , codex_responses dialect, oauth_external . Login is a device-code flow ( hermes_cli/auth.py:8204 ): CODEX_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID = \"app_EMoamEEZ73f0CkXaXp7hrann\" CODEX_OAUTH_TOKEN_URL = \"https://auth.openai.com/oauth/token\" CODEX_OAUTH_USER_AGENT = f\"hermes-cli/{__version__}\" The user opens auth.openai.com/codex/device , enters the code, and Hermes polls, then exchanges the authorization code. Tokens live at auth.json → providers.openai-codex.tokens ; Hermes only reads ~/.codex/auth.json , never writes it ( hermes_cli/auth.py:3830 ). Refresh has a 429→ CODEX_RATE_LIMITED path that keeps credentials valid, and a bespoke refresh_token_reused diagnostic when Codex CLI/VS Code consumed the single-use token ( hermes_cli/auth.py:3874 ). Edge-spoofing is required to pass Cloudflare in front of the Codex backend ( agent/auxiliary_client.py:1214 ): \"User-Agent\": \"codex_cli_rs (Hermes Agent)\", \"originator\": \"codex_cli_rs\", # + ChatGPT-Account-ID decoded from the OAuth JWT's chatgpt_account_id claim Non-residential IPs without an allowed originator get 403 cf-mitigated: challenge . Models are fetched live from /codex/models ( hermes_cli/codex_models.py:122 ); the fallback list includes the gpt-5.6-* / gpt-5.x-codex families. A banked-reset feature ( /usage reset ) redeems a credit to restore full 5h+weekly "},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"xAI Grok OAuth — SuperGrok / X Premium+ ( xai-oauth )","a":"grok","x":"xAI Grok OAuth — SuperGrok / X Premium+ ( xai-oauth ) Base https://api.x.ai/v1 , codex_responses transport, oauth_external . OIDC device-code flow ( hermes_cli/auth.py:8134 ): XAI_OAUTH_ISSUER = \"https://auth.x.ai\" XAI_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID = \"b1a00492-073a-47ea-816f-4c329264a828\" XAI_OAUTH_SCOPE = \"openid profile email offline_access grok-cli:access api:access\" XAI_ACCESS_TOKEN_REFRESH_SKEW_SECONDS = 3600 # tokens ~6h; refresh 1h early Refresh re-validates the cached token_endpoint against xAI's domain on every use — an auth.json written by an older Hermes could carry a non-xAI endpoint that would receive every future refresh token in plaintext ( hermes_cli/auth.py:4961 ). A 403 gets a dedicated xai_oauth_tier_denied message rather than a re-login prompt: xAI may restrict API/OAuth use to specific SuperGrok tiers despite an active in-app subscription ( hermes_cli/auth.py:4987 ). Unlike the others, xAI OAuth uses no spoofing — Hermes identifies honestly as Hermes-Agent/<version> ( tools/xai_http.py:95 ). Setup: hermes model → xAI Grok → OAuth, or hermes auth add xai-oauth ."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"GitHub Copilot — subscription via GitHub OAuth → short-lived JWT","a":"copilot","x":"GitHub Copilot — subscription via GitHub OAuth → short-lived JWT Base https://api.githubcopilot.com , dialect resolved per model (GPT-5+/Codex → responses, Claude → anthropic, rest → chat). Only gho_ / github_pat_ / ghu_ tokens are accepted — classic ghp_ is rejected ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:41 ). The device-code login uses VS Code's GitHub App client id, deliberately, because the old opencode App id produces tokens that can't be exchanged for Copilot JWTs ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:34 ): COPILOT_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID = \"Iv1.b507a08c87ecfe98\" The \"subscription contract\" is the token exchange ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:480 ): a GET to copilot_internal/v2/token with Editor-Version: vscode/1.104.1 returns a short-lived, semicolon-separated token plus the enterprise API endpoint, cached in-process and at ~/.hermes/.copilot_jwt.json , refreshed 120 s before expiry. Requests spoof the editor: Editor-Version: vscode/1.104.1 , User-Agent: HermesAgent/1.0 , Copilot-Integration-Id: vscode-chat , x-initiator: agent|user ( hermes_cli/copilot_auth.py:683 ). The sibling copilot-acp delegates auth entirely to a local copilot --acp subprocess logged in via copilot /login ( hermes_cli/model_setup_flows.py:1998 )."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Qwen OAuth — borrowed Qwen CLI subscription ( qwen-oauth )","a":"qwen","x":"Qwen OAuth — borrowed Qwen CLI subscription ( qwen-oauth ) Base https://portal.qwen.ai/v1 . Hermes has no login flow of its own : it reads ~/.qwen/oauth_creds.json written by qwen auth qwen-oauth and refuses with an instruction if absent ( hermes_cli/auth.py:2664 ). Refresh POSTs to chat.qwen.ai/api/v1/oauth2/token with client_id=\"f0304373b74a44d2b584a3fb70ca9e56\" , and writes the rotated pair back to the Qwen CLI's own file at 0600. Headers spoof the Qwen Code CLI: User-Agent: QwenCode/0.14.1 … plus DashScope headers ( run_agent.py:335 )."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"MiniMax OAuth — Coding Plan ( minimax-oauth )","a":"minimax","x":"MiniMax OAuth — Coding Plan ( minimax-oauth ) anthropic_messages dialect against .../anthropic . PKCE user-code flow ( hermes_cli/auth.py:8611 ): client_id=\"78257093-7e40-4613-99e0-527b14b39113\" , scope \"group_id profile model.completion\" , region-aware portals (global/CN). Tokens land in auth.json → providers.minimax-oauth ; setup via hermes model → MiniMax → OAuth or hermes auth add minimax-oauth --region cn|global ."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Nous Portal — first-party subscription ( nous )","a":"nous","x":"Nous Portal — first-party subscription ( nous ) Device-code OAuth against portal.nousresearch.com , client_id=\"hermes-cli\" , scope inference:invoke ( hermes_cli/auth.py:250 ). The access token doubles as the inference JWT, validated by scope + TTL before use. A subscription_required error at login prints the billing URL; entitlement/feature mapping lives in hermes_cli/nous_subscription.py . Cross-profile sharing goes through <root>/shared/nous_auth.json . Nous is dual-wire — anthropic/* catalog ids route to /v1/messages , everything else to /v1/chat/completions ( hermes_cli/providers.py:691 )."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Antigravity & the plan-key tiers","a":"antigravity","x":"Antigravity & the plan-key tiers Google Antigravity is not a provider in this tree. It exists only as an optional skill wrapping the local agy CLI — the skill's own note says it \"does not wrap a network API, so there is nothing to authenticate from Hermes itself\" ( optional-skills/autonomous-ai-agents/antigravity-cli/SKILL.md:17 ). Several vendors sell subscription plans that are consumed through a plain API key with special endpoint routing, so Hermes treats them as api_key providers: Z.AI / GLM Coding Plan : four official endpoints probed in priority order (a key that works on one returns \"Insufficient balance\" on another), winner cached by a key hash ( hermes_cli/auth.py:732 ). Kimi / Moonshot Coding Plan : sk-kimi- keys auto-route to api.kimi.com/coding ; on that host Hermes sends honest attribution headers rather than a spoof, at the Kimi team's request ( agent/anthropic_adapter.py:875 ). Alibaba Coding Plan , StepFun Step Plan , OpenCode Go ($10/mo key) — all dedicated endpoints keyed by a plan-tier API key."},{"u":"providers.html","p":"Providers & Tiers","t":"Setup surfaces","a":"surfaces","x":"Setup surfaces hermes model builds the provider picker from CANONICAL_PROVIDERS folded through groups (e.g. the \"openai\" group holds both openai-codex and openai-api ); every subscription provider has a bespoke flow, everything else falls through to a generic API-key flow ( hermes_cli/main.py:3913 ). hermes auth covers add | list | remove | reset | status | logout , with the OAuth-capable set fixed at {anthropic, nous, openai-codex, xai-oauth, qwen-oauth, minimax-oauth} ( hermes_cli/auth_commands.py:37 ). Removal dispatches to per-source teardown steps that decide suppress-vs-delete: Claude Code's file is suppressed, never deleted; Hermes-PKCE tokens are deleted outright ( agent/credential_sources.py:202 ). The single choke point for saving/removing an API key across the three stores ( .env , pool, config mirror) is hermes_cli/credential_lifecycle.py , whose contract only prunes env: -sourced entries and never touches OAuth blocks. Where Hermes actively impersonates another client. Only five: Anthropic OAuth ( claude-code UA + system prompt + product scrubbing + mcp__ renaming + axios/1.7.9 at the token endpoint), Codex ( codex_cli_rs originator), Copilot ( vscode/1.104.1 + GitHubCopilotChat ), and Qwen ( QwenCode/0.14.1 ). xAI, Nous, MiniMax and every API-key provider identify honestly as Hermes."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Messaging Channels","a":"","x":"One gateway process fronts every chat platform. Each channel is a platform adapter — a subclass of BasePlatformAdapter that normalizes inbound platform updates into a MessageEvent and sends replies back out. This page covers the quickest, cleanest setup for Telegram, WhatsApp (both the unofficial Baileys bridge and the official Meta Cloud API), and Twilio SMS, with the real config keys, plus a briefer tour of Discord, Slack and Signal."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"How a channel plugs into the gateway","a":"plug","x":"How a channel plugs into the gateway Adapters subclass BasePlatformAdapter and register through a plugin's register(ctx) → ctx.register_platform(...) ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:2774 ), which builds a PlatformEntry in the PlatformRegistry ( gateway/platform_registry.py:62 ). Heavy SDKs (python-telegram-bot, discord.py, slack_bolt) are lazy-loaded — a deferred loader per platform imports the SDK only when the registry is queried ( hermes_cli/plugins.py:4437 ). At startup GatewayRunner._create_adapter() checks the registry first, then falls through to a built-in if/elif chain ( gateway/run.py:15560 ). Enablement is env-driven — the mere presence of a token enables the channel ( gateway/config.py:1910 ff.): Telegram on TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN , WhatsApp/Baileys on WHATSAPP_ENABLED , WhatsApp Cloud on the phone-id + access-token pair, SMS/Twilio on TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID . YAML config in ~/.hermes/config.yaml is bridged into these env vars by each plugin's apply_yaml_config hook."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Authorization & pairing — one shared gate","a":"authz","x":"Authorization & pairing — one shared gate GatewayRunner._is_user_authorized() ( gateway/authz_mixin.py:386 ) applies one ordered policy across every channel: chat-scoped group allowlists → {PLATFORM}_ALLOW_BOTS → per-platform allow-all → the pairing store → env allowlists {PLATFORM}_ALLOWED_USERS + GATEWAY_ALLOWED_USERS → default deny . The global escape hatch is GATEWAY_ALLOW_ALL_USERS . The zero-config path is DM pairing : leave the allowlist unset, and an unknown DM gets a pairing code the operator approves with hermes pairing approve <platform> <code> — approval also writes the user into the platform's allowlist env var ( gateway/pairing.py:1 )."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Cross-cutting message handling","a":"crosscut","x":"Cross-cutting message handling Chunking : truncate_message() splits at natural boundaries, preserves/re-opens code fences, and appends (1/3) indicators; a len_fn lets Telegram measure in UTF-16 units ( gateway/platforms/base.py:7188 ). Typing : a _keep_typing() heartbeat refreshes every 2 s ( gateway/platforms/base.py:5104 ). Voice memos : gateway-side and provider-agnostic — _enrich_message_with_transcription() transcribes inbound audio and prepends it as quoted lines, optionally echoing 🎙️ \"…\" back to the chat ( gateway/run.py:24367 ). Cross-platform continuity : sessions are keyed per platform — there is no automatic shared session across platforms. The three bridges are /handoff <platform> (moves a live session keeping its id), the send_message tool with target=\"<platform>:<chat_id>\" , and delivery mirroring into the target session's transcript ( gateway/mirror.py:25 )."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Telegram","a":"telegram","x":"Telegram The most feature-complete adapter (~10.9k lines): TelegramAdapter over python-telegram-bot 22.8 ( plugins/platforms/telegram/adapter.py:638 ). Inbound is long polling by default , or webhook when TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL is set — and webhook mode refuses to start without TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET ( plugins/platforms/telegram/adapter.py:4640 ). Outbound sends MarkdownV2 with an automatic plain-text fallback when Telegram reports a parse error ( plugins/platforms/telegram/adapter.py:5255 ); the 4096-char limit is measured in UTF-16 code units. Voice memos route through the gateway STT pipeline; photos are cached locally so vision tools survive Telegram's ~1h ephemeral URLs. Quickest setup. Automatic (recommended) : hermes setup → Telegram → choice [1] does QR-based managed-bot onboarding through a Nous-hosted service and auto-detects your user id ( hermes_cli/telegram_managed_bot.py:21 ). Manual : /newbot in @BotFather for the token; message @userinfobot for your numeric id. Write ~/.hermes/.env : TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=… , TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS=<your id> , TELEGRAM_HOME_CHANNEL=<your id> (cron/notification target). Or leave ALLOWED_USERS unset and use pairing. For groups: disable BotFather privacy mode and re-add the bot . hermes gateway → expect [telegram] Connected to Telegram (polling mode) . Webhook mode (cloud/sleep-when-idle): add TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL and TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_"},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"WhatsApp via Baileys bridge (unofficial, personal accounts)","a":"whatsapp-baileys","x":"WhatsApp via Baileys bridge (unofficial, personal accounts) WhatsAppAdapter does not speak the WhatsApp protocol — it manages a Node.js sidecar ( Baileys @whiskeysockets/baileys ) and talks HTTP to 127.0.0.1:<bridge_port> ( plugins/platforms/whatsapp/adapter.py:381 , bridge at scripts/whatsapp-bridge/bridge.js:1 ). Inbound is 1-second HTTP polling of the local bridge's /messages ( plugins/platforms/whatsapp/adapter.py:1326 ); outbound is one POST /send per chunk. Pairing is by QR against WhatsApp's Linked Devices; the bridge serializes all sends to avoid cross-chat contamination. Quickest setup. Prereq: Node.js 18+. hermes whatsapp — choose mode (separate bot number vs personal self-chat), enter WHATSAPP_ALLOWED_USERS (country-code phone numbers, no + ; * = anyone), then scan the terminal QR: WhatsApp → Settings → Linked Devices → Link a Device ( hermes_cli/main.py:3257 ). Only after creds.json exists does it write WHATSAPP_ENABLED=true — a deliberate choice so an aborted wizard doesn't cost a 30 s gateway timeout. Alternative: pair from the web dashboard (streams QR frames). hermes gateway . Unofficial API — ban risk. Use a dedicated number."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"WhatsApp Business Cloud API (official Meta)","a":"whatsapp-cloud","x":"WhatsApp Business Cloud API (official Meta) WhatsAppCloudAdapter is built-in and talks raw HTTPS to the Meta Graph API via httpx, with an aiohttp webhook server for inbound ( gateway/platforms/whatsapp_cloud.py:203 ). Inbound is a webhook : a GET verify handshake plus a POST handler that validates the X-Hub-Signature-256 HMAC over the raw bytes and refuses with 503 when WHATSAPP_CLOUD_APP_SECRET is unset — the #1 \"inbound doesn't work\" cause ( gateway/platforms/whatsapp_cloud.py:1457 ). Typing + read receipt ship in one call (Meta couples them), and interactive button/list replies are supported. Note: free-form sends only within Meta's 24-hour window; groups not supported in v1. Quickest setup. Meta side: create an app → WhatsApp → API Setup → generate access token. hermes whatsapp-cloud — six validated prompts ( hermes_cli/setup_whatsapp_cloud.py:232 ): WHATSAPP_CLOUD_PHONE_NUMBER_ID (Meta's 15–17 digit id, not the phone number — the #1 trap), ACCESS_TOKEN ( EAA… ), APP_SECRET (32 hex), and an auto-generated VERIFY_TOKEN . Expose HTTPS: cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8090 . hermes gateway , then verify locally with a curl of the hub.challenge handshake before touching Meta. In Meta's dashboard set the callback URL <tunnel>/whatsapp/webhook + verify token, subscribe to the messages field, and add your phone to the recipient list."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Twilio SMS","a":"twilio","x":"Twilio SMS SmsAdapter uses no Twilio SDK — raw REST over aiohttp with HTTP Basic auth ( plugins/platforms/sms/adapter.py:81 ). Inbound is a webhook (Twilio POSTs form-encoded); the handler validates the X-Twilio-Signature HMAC-SHA1 (trying both with-port and without-port URL variants, since Twilio may sign either) and refuses to start without SMS_WEBHOOK_URL unless SMS_INSECURE_NO_SIGNATURE=true ( plugins/platforms/sms/adapter.py:322 ). Replies go out via the Twilio REST Messages.json API, not inline TwiML. Markdown is stripped (SMS renders it literally), the limit is 1600 chars (~10 segments), phone numbers are redacted in all logs, and MMS/media is not implemented. Quickest setup. Twilio Console: copy Account SID ( AC… ), Auth Token, and an SMS-capable number in E.164. uv pip install -e \".[sms]\" (installs aiohttp). ~/.hermes/.env : TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID , TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN , TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=+1… , and SMS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://your-host/webhooks/twilio (required), plus SMS_ALLOWED_USERS . Expose the port: cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8080 . Twilio Console → your number → Messaging → \"A message comes in\" → Webhook POST to <public>/webhooks/twilio . This URL must byte-match SMS_WEBHOOK_URL or every request fails signature validation. hermes gateway . Known bug (found during this survey). The SMS adapter's out-of-process sender ( _standalone_send , used by cron delive"},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"Discord / Slack / Signal (brief)","a":"others","x":"Discord / Slack / Signal (brief) Channel Library / transport Inbound Key env Discord discord.py[voice] 2.7.1 gateway websocket ( plugins/platforms/discord/adapter.py:1267 ) DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN , DISCORD_ALLOWED_USERS , DISCORD_HOME_CHANNEL ; 2000-char limit Slack slack-bolt in Socket Mode AsyncSocketModeHandler.start_async() ( plugins/platforms/slack/adapter.py:1228 ) SLACK_BOT_TOKEN ( xoxb- ), SLACK_APP_TOKEN ( xapp- ); hermes slack manifest generates the app manifest Signal signal-cli daemon; SSE inbound, JSON-RPC outbound SSE stream from the local daemon ( gateway/platforms/signal.py:254 ) SIGNAL_HTTP_URL , SIGNAL_ACCOUNT , SIGNAL_ALLOWED_USERS Beyond these, the repo ships plugin adapters for a2a, dingtalk, email, feishu, google_chat, homeassistant, irc, line, matrix, mattermost, ntfy, iMessage (photon/bluebubbles), teams, wecom and more, plus an experimental generic Relay adapter that fronts many platforms through one connector."},{"u":"channels.html","p":"Channels","t":"CLI reference","a":"commands","x":"CLI reference Command Purpose hermes setup Full first-run wizard incl. Telegram hermes gateway setup Per-platform picker (registry + built-ins) hermes whatsapp Baileys mode + npm install + QR pairing hermes whatsapp-cloud Meta Cloud API credential wizard hermes slack manifest Slack app manifest generator hermes pairing list|approve|revoke DM pairing approval hermes gateway Run the gateway daemon hermes status Shows configured platforms + home channels"},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Self-Adaptive Skills","a":"","x":"The marketing claim — \"creates skills from experience, improves them during use, nudges itself to persist knowledge, searches its own past conversations, builds a deepening model of who you are\" — maps to real, specific machinery. This page documents the workflows that transform work-over-time into skills: the counter that decides a task was complex, the background fork that writes skills without touching the live conversation, the ownership guards that keep the agent from mutating what it doesn't own, and the weekly curator that consolidates the library."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"The five loops","a":"five-loops","x":"The five loops Loop Trigger Actor Writes to A. Post-turn review turn / tool-iteration counter forked AIAgent , tools = memory + skills ~/.hermes/skills/** , MEMORY.md , USER.md B. /refine user command same fork + focus same C. /learn user command the live foreground agent ~/.hermes/skills/** D. Curator 7-day interval + idle gate deterministic pass + optional fork lifecycle state, .archive/ , umbrellas E. Honcho dialectic every turn (cadenced) Honcho cloud peer external workspace, injected into context The design intent is stated in the review module's own docstring: \"After every turn, run_conversation may fire off a daemon thread that replays the conversation snapshot in a forked AIAgent and asks itself 'should any skill/memory be saved or updated?'. Writes go straight to the memory + skill stores. Main conversation and prompt cache are never touched.\" ( agent/background_review.py:1 )."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Skill format & storage","a":"format","x":"Skill format & storage Three shipped trees plus one runtime tree: skills/ (82 bundled SKILL.md files), optional-skills/ (117, installed on demand), and the user tree ~/.hermes/skills/ ( hermes_constants.py:1515 ). The format is agentskills.io-compatible — YAML frontmatter + Markdown body, with an assets/ / references/ / templates/ / scripts/ layout ( tools/skills_tool.py:28 ; allowed subdirs whitelisted at tools/skill_manager_tool.py:520 ). A hard validator enforces frontmatter shape and — on create only — a description ≤ 60 characters ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:566 ). That 60-char limit is the single highest-leverage constraint in the system: it gates creation, is repeated in three separate prompts, has its own linter rule, and its violation silently truncates the description so the skill never routes ( agent/learn_prompt.py:44 ). A 12-rule advisory linter runs after every create ( tools/skill_linter.py:1 ). Seeding never clobbers user edits: a bundled skill that changed and whose user copy diverged is skipped ( tools/skills_sync.py:13 )."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"How skills reach the model's context — three tiers","a":"context","x":"How skills reach the model's context — three tiers Always-on index in the system prompt. A ## Skills (mandatory) block lists every skill's name + 60-char description and instructs: scan before replying, load with skill_view(name) if even partially relevant, patch it if it has issues, and offer to save one after difficult tasks ( agent/prompt_builder.py:2101 ). The self-improvement instruction is baked into the always-loaded index itself. Full body via skill_view . Loading a skill bumps both view and use telemetry — actively loading a skill to act on it counts as use, and the curator's stale timer keys off last_used_at ( tools/skills_tool.py:2135 ). Support files on demand via skill_view(name, file_path=\"references/x.md\") . The index sits in the volatile prompt band, ahead of memory, precisely because skills are runtime-mutable — a stable-band placement would bust the cached prefix on every self-edit ( agent/system_prompt.py:765 ). There is no embedding or semantic router : matching is the model reading descriptions plus deterministic frontmatter filters (platform gating, tool-dependency gating, disabled lists)."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Autonomous skill creation — the trigger","a":"creation","x":"Autonomous skill creation — the trigger \"This task was complex\" is operationalized as ≥N tool iterations since the last skill_manage call . The counter _iters_since_skill increments once per tool-calling iteration ( agent/conversation_loop.py:1988 ), resets when skill_manage is actually used ( agent/tool_executor.py:667 ), and is checked at turn end against skills.creation_nudge_interval (default 10 in code, 15 in the example config) ( agent/turn_finalizer.py:770 ). The spawn gate additionally requires a real final response, no interrupt, not a cron session, not a subagent, and the feature enabled ( run_agent.py:1832 ). The prompt ( _SKILL_REVIEW_PROMPT , agent/background_review.py:259 ) is emphatic and specific — a few load-bearing excerpts: \"Be ACTIVE — most sessions produce at least one skill update… A pass that does nothing is a missed learning opportunity, not a neutral outcome.\" \"Target shape of the library: CLASS-LEVEL skills, each with a rich SKILL.md and a references/ directory… Not a long flat list of narrow one-session-one-skill entries.\" Preference order, earliest that fits: (1) update a currently-loaded skill, (2) update an existing umbrella, (3) add a support file, (4) create a new class-level umbrella. \"If the proposed name only makes sense for today's task, it's wrong.\" And a negative half — the anti-sediment rules — that refuses to capture environment-dependent"},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"What the review fork actually is","a":"the-fork","x":"What the review fork actually is _run_review_in_thread ( agent/background_review.py:860 ) builds a forked AIAgent with a stack of invariants, each fixing a real bug: Property Why max_iterations=16 , tool whitelist {memory, skills} bounded cost; other tools denied at runtime skip_memory=True the fork must not touch external memory providers (three leak sites) _persist_disabled=True , _session_db=None the curator-takeover root cause : otherwise the harness prompt is written into the user's real session and re-read next turn as a standing instruction, making the agent \"become\" the curator cached system prompt inherited (same model) prefix-cache hit — cited ~26% end-to-end cost reduction compression_enabled=False sharing session_id means a compression race would fork the parent dangerous-command approvals auto-denied prevents an input() deadlock against the TUI Same-model reviews replay the full snapshot; a routed cheaper model replays a compact digest instead. Writes land on disk, provenance is stamped in .usage.json , an audit entry lands in .curator_ledger.jsonl , the skills prompt cache is cleared so the just-written skill is visible next turn, and the user sees 💾 Self-improvement review: <actions> ."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Skill self-improvement during use","a":"improve","x":"Skill self-improvement during use There is no critique loop and no semver bumping — self-improvement is instruction plus telemetry: The system prompt's SKILLS_GUIDANCE : \"When using a skill and finding it outdated, incomplete, or wrong, patch it immediately with skill_manage(action='patch') — don't wait to be asked. Skills that aren't maintained become liabilities.\" ( agent/prompt_builder.py:212 ). The tool schema declares patch the preferred action; edit is for major overhauls only. Foreground path : the live agent calls skill_manage(action='patch', old_string, new_string) on the skill it just used — a unique-match requirement guards it ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:1069 ). Autonomous path : the end-of-turn fork re-reads the loaded skill with skill_view , then patches — only if the skill passes the ownership guards below. \"Versioning\" is a reuse-after-patch signal in telemetry: bump_patch increments patch_generation , and a subsequent successful use flips reuse_after_patch true, evidencing the improvement stuck ( tools/skill_usage.py:864 ). Recoverability comes from the per-mutation ledger and pre-run tarball snapshots, not frontmatter versions."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"The ownership guards — how the agent can only improve what it owns","a":"guards","x":"The ownership guards — how the agent can only improve what it owns The autonomous half prefers UPDATE over CREATE, made safe by five guards ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:301 ): Ownership guard — no-op for foreground agents; for the fork it refuses, in order: pinned, external-dir, protected builtin, hub-installed, bundled, and finally not-curator-managed. A missing telemetry record and an explicit created_by: null resolve identically — otherwise \"allowed exactly once\" would be a race with the guard's own bookkeeping. The refusal names the escape hatch: hermes curator adopt <name> . Read-before-write guard — the fork may only patch a path it actually skill_view 'd this turn, never content inferred from the transcript ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:424 ). Consolidation-delete guard — during a review/curator pass, delete requires a non-empty absorbed_into=<umbrella> ; a past fail-open pass once archived whole clusters of active skills ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:463 ). Delete → archive , never rmtree , during autonomous passes (recoverable .archive/ ). Org-mirror guard — org-shared skills are editable (edits go to a personal mirror and reach the org via hermes sync propose ) but not deletable. The docstring is the clearest statement of the whole philosophy: blocking every write \"broke the learning loop exactly where it matters most\" ( tools/skill_manager_tool.py:699 ). Provenance "},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Memory nudges","a":"memory","x":"Memory nudges _turns_since_memory increments per user turn, hydrates from persisted history on resume so a restart doesn't reset the clock, and at memory.nudge_interval (default 10) arms should_review_memory ( agent/turn_context.py:703 ). The nudge is not injected into the conversation — the prologue explicitly preserves the original user message; the flag rides through to the finalizer, which spawns the same fork. The prompt ( _MEMORY_REVIEW_PROMPT ) is short: save persona/preferences/personal details and expectations about how the agent should behave ( agent/background_review.py:248 ). Memory is two Markdown files , not SQLite: ~/.hermes/memories/MEMORY.md (agent notes) and USER.md (user profile), delimited by § , bounded by character limits ( tools/memory_tool.py:53 ). The load-bearing design is the frozen snapshot : files are injected at session start; mid-session writes update disk immediately (durable) but do not change the system prompt, preserving the prefix cache for the whole session ( tools/memory_tool.py:11 ). Guidance draws a sharp line: reusable procedures belong in a skill , task progress belongs in session search , and memories must be declarative facts (\"User prefers concise responses\" ✓) not imperatives (\"Always respond concisely\" ✗)."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Session search / cross-session recall","a":"search","x":"Session search / cross-session recall Transcripts live in the SQLite state DB with an FTS5 external-content index plus a trigram index for CJK/substring search ( hermes_state_common.py:483 ). The agent-facing tool makes no LLM calls — every shape returns actual DB rows ( tools/session_search_tool.py:1128 ): DISCOVERY (FTS with adaptive hydration of only the top hit), SCROLL (a window around a message), READ (a whole session), BROWSE (recent). Notable behaviors: cron sessions demoted below interactive results to fix \"recall blindness\" where a high-volume cron corpus starved user sessions under bare BM25; lineage dedup so compression continuations don't return the same conversation N times; and an explicit anti-hallucination clause forbidding \"no prior correspondence\" conclusions from search alone when a direct source was given. The \"LLM summarization\" lives adjacent, not in the search path: LLM-generated session titles ( agent/title_generator.py:74 ) that are themselves searchable, plus compaction summaries written into the message stream and therefore FTS-indexed. SESSION_SEARCH_GUIDANCE tells the model to recall before asking the user to repeat themselves."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Honcho dialectic user modeling","a":"honcho","x":"Honcho dialectic user modeling An optional external provider ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:262 ) selected by memory.provider . Per turn: on_turn_start → prefetch_all (base context + a dialectic supplement, each on its own cadence) → the recalled text is composed into the API copy of the user message only, wrapped in a <memory-context> block marked \"recalled memory context, NOT new user input\", and stamped as api_content so it never enters the clean transcript → sync_all + queue_prefetch_all on a serialized background worker so a wedged provider can't stall the turn. The dialectic itself runs up to three peer.chat() passes — a cold/warm opening question, a self-audit (\"what gaps remain?\"), and a reconciliation (\"do these cohere?\") — bailing early on strong signal ( plugins/memory/honcho/__init__.py:1170 ). It renders up to five blocks — Session Summary, User Representation, User Peer Card, AI Self-Representation, AI Identity Card — the literal \"deepening model of who you are\". The background review fork is built with skip_memory=True precisely so it never writes the harness prompt into the user's real Honcho namespace."},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"The curator — the slower library-level loop","a":"curator","x":"The curator — the slower library-level loop Not a cron daemon — it is polled at CLI startup and in the gateway housekeeping loop ( agent/curator.py:1 ). It runs when curator.enabled ∧ not paused ∧ last_run_at older than interval_hours (default 7 days), with an idle gate. Two passes: Pass 1 — deterministic, no LLM. Walks usage: active → stale after 30 days idle, → archived after 90, reactivating on re-use. Skips pinned, cron-referenced, and young-unused skills (\"a use=0 skill is absence of evidence, not evidence of staleness\") ( agent/curator.py:305 ). Pass 2 — LLM consolidation, OFF by default ( curator.consolidate: False ). When on, it forks an agent with the CURATOR_REVIEW_PROMPT ( agent/curator.py:432 ) to build umbrella skills — cluster prefix-related skills, merge into or create umbrellas, demote narrow siblings to references/ . \"One broad umbrella skill with labeled subsections beats five narrow siblings for discoverability.\" It requires a structured YAML summary listing every archived skill, reconciled against what actually happened on disk, with a pre-run tarball snapshot as a safety net. The adopt CLI is the documented escape hatch the refusals point at, and its own docstring explains why adoption can never be automatic: \"a high patch count proves the agent MAINTAINS a skill, not that it AUTHORED it\" ( hermes_cli/curator.py:344 ). The plan skill is a protected builtin,"},{"u":"skills.html","p":"Self-Adaptive Skills","t":"Honest caveats for the reader","a":"caveats","x":"Honest caveats for the reader Nudges are counters, not prompt injections — the live conversation never sees them. LLM consolidation is off by default — out of the box a curator run does only the deterministic prune; the dramatic curator prompt runs only when opted in. No embeddings anywhere in skill routing — 60-char descriptions + frontmatter filters. FTS5 is keyword/BM25/trigram, not vector. \"LLM summarization\" for session search = LLM-generated session titles + indexed compaction summaries; the search tool makes no LLM calls. Skill \"versioning\" is telemetry + ledger + tarball snapshots, not semver frontmatter. hermes_cli/curator.py curates skills , not memory; memory consolidation is forced by the char limit and done by the model in-band."},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Transferable Ideas","a":"","x":"This page is the \"what can be transferred\" index: the best methods, algorithms and hard-won know-how in Hermes that generalize to any serious agent harness, regardless of framework. Each item names the idea, the concrete problem it solves, and the file to read. The recurring theme is that the non-obvious win is almost always about protecting the model's context window, surviving real-world rate limits, or making stateless infrastructure behave like stateful shells."},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"The recurring thesis","a":"thesis","x":"The recurring thesis Read enough of the codebase and one pattern dominates: the clever code is not about model orchestration, it is about defending three scarce resources. Almost every non-obvious design choice traces back to one of them. Scarce resource What breaks without care Where Hermes defends it Context window tool outputs bloat the prompt; compaction thrashes; caches break RPC tool calls, structured compaction, cache-stable serialization Rate / tier quota SDK double-retries, credential races, one 429 stalls the fleet owned retry loop, single-use-token flocks, credential pool Stateless infra serverless sandboxes lose the filesystem between turns filesystem-snapshot persistence, self-suspend, wake"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Protecting the context window","a":"context","x":"Protecting the context window Idea Problem solved Read RPC zero-context tool calls ( execute_code ) The model writes a Python script that calls real tools over an RPC stub, so a 50-call pipeline costs one inference turn and intermediate outputs never enter context. The iteration is even refunded to the budget. tools/code_execution_tool.py:652 Compression uses prompt tokens only Reasoning tokens inflate completion counts and caused premature compaction. Always trigger on the provider's real prompt count, with a schema-inclusive rough estimate as fallback (50+ tools add 20–30K tokens). agent/conversation_loop.py:7305 Rearm compaction only on confirmed recovery A completed compaction clears the attempt budget only when the very next request's real prompt count proves it dropped below threshold — not optimistically. agent/conversation_loop.py:4021 Information-bearing compaction Replace old tool outputs with typed one-liners (not dumb placeholders), dedup identical results by content hash, and repair orphaned tool_call/result pairs so the provider never 400s. agent/context_compressor.py:1848 Frozen memory snapshot Memory files are injected at session start; mid-session writes hit disk immediately but do not change the system prompt, preserving the KV-cache prefix for the whole session. tools/memory_tool.py:11 Cache-stable serialization Re-serialize tool-call JSON with sort_keys=True"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Surviving real rate limits","a":"rate","x":"Surviving real rate limits Idea Problem solved Read Own the retry loop; disable SDK retries Every SDK's max_retries=0 , because the SDK default ignores Retry-After and double-retries inside your own loop. All backoff, rotation and fallback live in one place. run_agent.py:5331 Distinguish account-429 from capacity-429 Inspect the 429's own x-ratelimit-* headers: an account-level limit needs backoff or credential rotation, an upstream-capacity limit needs a plain retry. agent/conversation_loop.py:5347 Single-use refresh-token flock Codex and xAI rotate refresh tokens on use; wrap refresh in a cross-process auth.json flock and re-read the live store first so two processes don't burn each other's token. agent/credential_pool.py:1313 Credential pool with rotation strategies Multiple accounts per provider ( fill_first | round_robin | random | least_used ), rotated before falling back to a different model — so one exhausted subscription doesn't stop the agent. agent/credential_pool.py:164 Banked quota reset Model the provider's own \"restore full limits\" credit, and refuse to spend it when the busiest window isn't near full — spending early wastes most of it. agent/account_usage.py:587 Always-stream health checks Prefer streaming even with no consumer, purely to gain the 90s stale-stream and 60s read-timeout detectors the non-streaming path lacks; repackage into a non-streaming shape s"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Concurrency & identity in a multi-tenant process","a":"concurrency","x":"Concurrency & identity in a multi-tenant process Idea Problem solved Read Routing identity in ContextVars, copied at every thread boundary Concurrent gateway messages were overwriting each other's os.environ routing identity. Move identity into ContextVar s with a _UNSET sentinel distinguishing \"never set\" from \"explicitly cleared\", and copy the context at every hand-off. One of the cleanest fixes in the repo. gateway/session_context.py:11 Turn lease on the resolved session_id Serialize turns on the resolved id, not the routing key, so two keys mapping to one session can't run concurrently. Fail closed on timeout. gateway/turn_lease.py:191 Clear the guard, never delete it On drain, .clear() the per-session Event but never delete the entry — deleting admits a second concurrent agent on the same key. gateway/platforms/base.py:6768 Tail-recurse the turn chain, but hand off via fresh tasks Follow-ups recurse with a depth cap (3); the adapter-level drain spawns a fresh task rather than awaiting, because the await chain grew one C-stack frame per follow-up and segfaulted at ~2000. gateway/platforms/base.py:6765 Daemon pools + detach wedged workers Tool workers run on a daemon pool (a wedged non-daemon thread blocks interpreter exit); on abandon, shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True) leaves a hung tool detached rather than deadlocking the batch. agent/tool_executor.py:1622 Start-o"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"The closed learning loop","a":"learning","x":"The closed learning loop Idea Problem solved Read Complexity = tool-iteration count Operationalize \"this task was hard, save a skill\" as ≥N tool iterations since the last skill write — a cheap, robust proxy that needs no classifier. agent/turn_finalizer.py:770 Out-of-band review fork Do self-improvement in a forked agent with _persist_disabled so the harness prompt never enters the user's real session (the \"agent becomes the curator\" bug). Inherit the cached system prompt for a warm cache. Cancel the prior fork before starting a new one. agent/background_review.py:860 Provenance-gated self-editing A per-turn ContextVar marks writes as user-directed vs autonomous, so the curator only ever mutates skills the agent authored — user skills stay the user's. Missing record and explicit null resolve identically to avoid a race with your own bookkeeping. tools/skill_provenance.py:3 Read-before-write for autonomous edits The review fork may only patch content it actually loaded this turn, never content inferred from the transcript. tools/skill_manager_tool.py:424 Anti-sediment capture rules Explicitly refuse to persist environment-dependent failures and negative tool claims — they \"harden into refusals the agent cites against itself for months after the actual problem was fixed.\" agent/background_review.py:332 Umbrella-building curator A slow, polled consolidation pass that clusters narr"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Safety & trust boundaries","a":"safety","x":"Safety & trust boundaries Idea Problem solved Read Untrusted output framed as data Wrap results from web/browser/MCP tools in semantic delimiters marking them \"data, not instructions\", at one chokepoint — the architectural defense against indirect prompt injection — and teach the model a one-shot freshness rule for the trusted steer marker. agent/tool_dispatch_helpers.py:534 Never serialize raw chain-of-thought Inlining an assistant's own CoT into replayable content reads to provider classifiers as a prefill jailbreak and bricks the session with deterministic empty-response storms. Keep interrupt scaffold text out of assistant content too. agent/conversation_loop.py:268 Persist before side effects Flush the assistant tool-call row to SQLite before any tool executes — a destructive tool can restart the process, and resume logic must see the exact block that ran. A flush failure aborts the turn rather than executing from process-local state. agent/conversation_loop.py:7161 Socket-shutdown, not client-close, from stranger threads A watchdog must never close() a client whose TLS socket another thread is reading; strangers only shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) , owners do the SDK close — closing a real memory-corruption class. run_agent.py:5429 Auxiliary-LLM approval guardian A guardian LLM inside the approval gate triages approve/deny/escalate before a human is bothered — borrowed from Codex's "},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"Making stateless infra behave statefully","a":"serverless","x":"Making stateless infra behave statefully Idea Problem solved Read Filesystem-snapshot persistence Serverless sandboxes (Modal, Daytona) lose the filesystem between turns; snapshot on cleanup and restore next session to fake persistence on hibernating sandboxes. Internals report Self-suspend, don't trust the proxy The gateway self-suspends via the Fly Machines socket because the Fly proxy judges idleness on inbound connections only and cannot see an in-flight turn. gateway/scale_to_zero.py:1 Push-or-self-POST wake Push-capable adapters wake via a synthetic internal event; stateless adapters get a self-POST carrying the raw session id, because a derived key would land the wake in an invisible parallel session. gateway/wake.py:1 Idle-based timeouts, not wall-clock The watchdog fires on idle seconds, so a legitimately busy multi-hour turn is never killed — activity is stamped at every meaningful point in the loop. gateway/run.py:3320"},{"u":"transfer.html","p":"Transferables","t":"What to lift first","a":"pick","x":"What to lift first If you are building your own harness and want the highest-leverage ideas per unit of effort: RPC tool calls ( execute_code ) — the single biggest context-window win; collapses multi-step pipelines to one turn. Own your retry loop, disable the SDK's — a small change that fixes the most common production failure (double-retried 429s that ignore Retry-After ). ContextVar routing identity with copies at every thread boundary — the correct answer to multi-tenant agent identity leaks. Persist-before-side-effects + one-result-per-tool-call — the invariants that make resume and provider replay actually work. Untrusted-output-as-data framing — the cheapest meaningful defense against indirect prompt injection. Out-of-band review fork with _persist_disabled — if you want a learning loop, this is the structural trick that keeps it from poisoning the live session. The meta-lesson. Nearly every citation on this page is accompanied in the source by a comment naming a specific bug number and the failure it caused. The transferable asset is not just the code — it is the catalog of failure modes a production agent hits, and the minimal invariant that closes each one."}];
